window.interviewData = [
  {
    id: "fundamentals",
    title: "1. Kubernetes Fundamentals",
    questions: [
      { id: 1, question: "What is Kubernetes?", answer: "<p>Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications across clusters of hosts. Originally designed by Google, it is now maintained by the CNCF (Cloud Native Computing Foundation).</p>" },
      { id: 2, question: "Explain the Kubernetes architecture.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes has two main layers:</p><ul><li><strong>Control Plane:</strong> API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager — manages cluster state.</li><li><strong>Data Plane (Worker Nodes):</strong> kubelet, kube-proxy, Container Runtime — runs workloads.</li></ul><p>The kubectl CLI and external tools communicate only with the API Server, which is the single entry point to the control plane.</p><img src='assets/k8s_architecture.png' alt='Kubernetes Architecture' style='width:100%;max-width:600px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;border:1px solid var(--border-color);'>" },
      { id: 3, question: "What are the responsibilities of the API Server?", answer: "<p>The <code>kube-apiserver</code> is the front-end of the Kubernetes control plane. Its responsibilities include:</p><ul><li>Exposing the Kubernetes REST API</li><li>Authentication and Authorization of all requests</li><li>The <em>only</em> component that reads/writes to etcd</li><li>Admission control (validating and mutating webhooks)</li><li>API versioning and schema validation</li></ul>" },
      { id: 4, question: "What does the Scheduler do?", answer: "<p>The <code>kube-scheduler</code> watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node and selects an appropriate node for them. Scheduling decisions are based on: resource requirements (CPU, memory), hardware/software constraints, node affinity/anti-affinity rules, taints and tolerations, and data locality.</p>" },
      { id: 5, question: "What is the Controller Manager?", answer: "<p>The <code>kube-controller-manager</code> runs a set of controller loops, each responsible for a specific cluster function. Examples include:</p><ul><li><strong>Node Controller:</strong> Monitors node health</li><li><strong>ReplicaSet Controller:</strong> Maintains correct pod replica counts</li><li><strong>Deployment Controller:</strong> Manages rolling updates</li><li><strong>EndpointSlice Controller:</strong> Populates endpoint objects</li></ul>" },
      { id: 6, question: "What is etcd, and why is it important?", answer: "<p><code>etcd</code> is a distributed, strongly consistent key-value store that Kubernetes uses as its backing store for all cluster data. It stores the desired state of every object (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps, etc.). If etcd data is lost and unrecoverable, the entire cluster state is gone — making regular backups critical.</p>" },
      { id: 7, question: "What is a Pod?", answer: "<p>A Pod is the smallest and most basic deployable unit in Kubernetes. It wraps one or more containers that share:</p><ul><li>The same network namespace (same IP address and port space)</li><li>Storage volumes</li><li>Lifecycle (start/stop together)</li></ul><p>Think of a Pod as a logical host for tightly coupled processes.</p>" },
      { id: 8, question: "Why shouldn't you run multiple unrelated containers in one Pod?", answer: "<p>Pods are the unit of scaling in Kubernetes — you can't scale containers inside a Pod independently. If you colocate a frontend and a database in one Pod, you are forced to scale both together, waste resources, and lose independent lifecycle management. Use separate Pods or a sidecar pattern only for tightly-coupled helpers.</p>" },
      { id: 9, question: "What is a ReplicaSet?", answer: "<p>A ReplicaSet ensures that a specified number of Pod replicas are running at any given time. If a Pod crashes, the ReplicaSet creates a new one. However, ReplicaSets are rarely managed directly — instead, you use a <strong>Deployment</strong> which manages ReplicaSets for you and adds update/rollback capabilities.</p>" },
      { id: 10, question: "How is a Deployment different from a ReplicaSet?", answer: "<p>A Deployment is a higher-level abstraction that owns and manages ReplicaSets. Key differences:</p><ul><li>Deployments support <strong>rolling updates</strong> and <strong>rollbacks</strong></li><li>When you update a Deployment spec, it creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually migrates Pods to it</li><li>Old ReplicaSets are kept (by default 10) allowing rollback</li><li>ReplicaSets alone cannot perform updates</li></ul>" },
      { id: 11, question: "When would you use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment?", answer: "<p>Use a StatefulSet when your application requires:</p><ul><li><strong>Stable, unique network identities</strong> (e.g., <code>pod-0</code>, <code>pod-1</code>)</li><li><strong>Stable, persistent storage</strong> — each Pod gets its own PVC that follows it across rescheduling</li><li><strong>Ordered, graceful deployment and scaling</strong></li></ul><p>Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch, Kafka, Zookeeper.</p>" },
      { id: 12, question: "What is a DaemonSet?", answer: "<p>A DaemonSet ensures that exactly one copy of a Pod runs on every node in the cluster (or a subset based on selectors). When new nodes are added, DaemonSet Pods are automatically scheduled there. Common uses: log collectors (Fluentd/Fluent Bit), monitoring agents (Prometheus Node Exporter), network plugins (kube-proxy), storage drivers.</p>" },
      { id: 13, question: "What are Jobs and CronJobs?", answer: "<p><strong>Job:</strong> Creates one or more Pods and ensures a specified number successfully terminate (run-to-completion). Used for batch processing, data migrations, one-off tasks.</p><p><strong>CronJob:</strong> Schedules Jobs on a time-based schedule (like Linux cron). Example: <code>0 2 * * *</code> runs a Job at 2 AM daily.</p>" },
      { id: 14, question: "What are Init Containers?", answer: "<p>Init Containers run and complete <em>before</em> the main application containers start in a Pod. They are used for initialization tasks such as:</p><ul><li>Waiting for a database or service to become ready</li><li>Downloading configuration or credentials</li><li>Setting up filesystem permissions</li><li>Seeding data</li></ul><p>If any init container fails, Kubernetes restarts the Pod.</p>" },
      { id: 15, question: "What are Sidecar containers?", answer: "<p>A sidecar is an additional container in a Pod that extends or enhances the main application container without modifying it. They share the same network and storage. Common use cases:</p><ul><li>Logging proxy (Fluent Bit)</li><li>Service mesh proxy (Envoy/Istio)</li><li>Secret/credential fetcher</li><li>Metrics exporter</li></ul>" },
      { id: 16, question: "Explain Kubernetes Services.", answer: "<p>A Service provides a stable network endpoint (ClusterIP + DNS name) to expose a set of Pods. Since Pods are ephemeral and their IPs change, a Service acts as a stable abstraction that load-balances traffic across all healthy matching Pods using label selectors.</p>" },
      { id: 17, question: "Difference between ClusterIP, NodePort, and LoadBalancer.", answer: "<ul><li><strong>ClusterIP (default):</strong> Exposes the service only within the cluster. No external access.</li><li><strong>NodePort:</strong> Exposes the service on a static port (30000-32767) on every node's IP. Accessible from outside the cluster.</li><li><strong>LoadBalancer:</strong> Provisions an external cloud load balancer (e.g., AWS NLB/ALB) with a public IP, routing traffic to the NodePort. Requires a cloud provider or MetalLB.</li></ul>" },
      { id: 18, question: "What is an Ingress?", answer: "<p>An Ingress is a Kubernetes API object that manages external HTTP/HTTPS access to Services. It provides:</p><ul><li>Layer 7 (HTTP) routing based on path and hostname</li><li>SSL/TLS termination</li><li>Name-based virtual hosting</li></ul><p>An <strong>Ingress Controller</strong> (NGINX, AWS ALB, Traefik) must be running in the cluster to process Ingress resources.</p>" },
      { id: 19, question: "Difference between Ingress and Gateway API.", answer: "<p>Ingress is the legacy, simpler L7 API. The <strong>Kubernetes Gateway API</strong> is the modern, role-oriented successor providing:</p><ul><li>L4 and L7 support (TCP, UDP, HTTP, gRPC)</li><li>Cross-namespace traffic routing</li><li>Better separation of concerns (Infrastructure / Cluster Operator / Developer roles)</li><li>More expressive routing rules</li></ul>" },
      { id: 20, question: "What are ConfigMaps?", answer: "<p>ConfigMaps store non-confidential configuration data as key-value pairs. Pods can consume them as:</p><ul><li>Environment variables</li><li>Command-line arguments</li><li>Files mounted in a volume</li></ul><p>ConfigMaps decouple environment-specific configuration from container images, enabling the same image to run in dev/staging/prod.</p>" },
      { id: 21, question: "What are Secrets?", answer: "<p>Secrets are similar to ConfigMaps but designed for sensitive data (passwords, API keys, TLS certs). They are base64-encoded (not encrypted) by default. In production:</p><ul><li>Enable <strong>Encryption at Rest</strong> via KMS in etcd</li><li>Use <strong>Secrets Store CSI Driver</strong> or <strong>External Secrets Operator</strong> to pull from AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault</li></ul>" },
      { id: 22, question: "What are Namespaces?", answer: "<p>Namespaces provide a mechanism for isolating groups of resources within a single cluster, acting as virtual sub-clusters. They are used to:</p><ul><li>Separate environments (dev/staging/prod)</li><li>Isolate teams or tenants</li><li>Apply ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges</li><li>Scope RBAC policies</li></ul>" },
      { id: 23, question: "What are Labels and Selectors?", answer: "<p><strong>Labels</strong> are key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects. They identify and group resources without affecting API semantics. <strong>Selectors</strong> filter objects by their labels. Services, Deployments, and Controllers all use label selectors to identify which Pods they manage.</p>" },
      { id: 24, question: "What are Annotations?", answer: "<p>Annotations are key-value pairs that attach arbitrary non-identifying metadata to objects. Unlike labels, they are not used for selection. Common uses include storing deployment tool info, build IDs, monitoring configurations, or Ingress controller settings (e.g., <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class: alb</code>).</p>" },
      { id: 25, question: "Explain the Kubernetes reconciliation loop.", answer: "<p>The control loop is the heart of Kubernetes. Controllers continuously:</p><ol><li><strong>Observe</strong> the current actual state of the cluster</li><li><strong>Compare</strong> it to the desired state stored in etcd</li><li><strong>Act</strong> to make the actual state match the desired state</li></ol><p>This idempotent loop means Kubernetes is always self-healing and eventually consistent.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "scheduling",
    title: "2. Pods & Scheduling",
    questions: [
      { id: 26, question: "How does Kubernetes schedule Pods?", answer: "<p>Scheduling happens in two phases:</p><ul><li><strong>Filtering:</strong> Eliminates nodes that don't meet the Pod's requirements (insufficient resources, taints, nodeSelector mismatch).</li><li><strong>Scoring:</strong> Ranks the remaining nodes by priority (least requested resources, affinity preferences, spread).</li></ul><p>The Pod is bound to the highest-scoring node.</p>" },
      { id: 27, question: "Explain nodeSelector.", answer: "<p><code>nodeSelector</code> is the simplest node constraint. You define a map of labels, and the Pod will only be scheduled onto nodes with those matching labels. Example: <code>disktype: ssd</code> schedules only on SSD nodes. It's an equality-based selector; for richer rules, use Node Affinity.</p>" },
      { id: 28, question: "What is Node Affinity?", answer: "<p>Node Affinity is a more expressive replacement for nodeSelector. It supports:</p><ul><li><strong>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</strong> Hard rule — node must match or Pod won't schedule.</li><li><strong>preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</strong> Soft rule — scheduler tries to match but will schedule elsewhere if needed.</li></ul><p>Supports operators: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>Gt</code>, <code>Lt</code>.</p>" },
      { id: 29, question: "What is Pod Affinity?", answer: "<p>Pod Affinity constrains which nodes a Pod can be scheduled on, based on labels of <em>other Pods already running on that node</em>. This ensures related Pods (e.g., a web app and its cache) are co-located on the same node or AZ for lower latency.</p>" },
      { id: 30, question: "What is Pod Anti-Affinity?", answer: "<p>Pod Anti-Affinity is the opposite — it ensures Pods are scheduled <em>away</em> from specific other Pods. Commonly used to spread replicas of the same Deployment across different nodes or Availability Zones for high availability, preventing a single node failure from taking down all replicas.</p>" },
      { id: 31, question: "What are Taints?", answer: "<p>Taints are applied to <strong>nodes</strong> to repel Pods. A tainted node will not accept any Pod unless the Pod explicitly tolerates the taint. Effects: <code>NoSchedule</code> (won't schedule), <code>PreferNoSchedule</code> (tries to avoid), <code>NoExecute</code> (evicts existing Pods). Used to dedicate nodes for specific workloads (e.g., GPU nodes).</p>" },
      { id: 32, question: "What are Tolerations?", answer: "<p>Tolerations are applied to <strong>Pods</strong>. They allow a Pod to be scheduled onto nodes with matching taints. A toleration says: 'I can tolerate this taint.' Important: tolerations allow but do not require scheduling on tainted nodes — you still need Node Affinity to force placement.</p>" },
      { id: 33, question: "Explain Priority Classes.", answer: "<p>PriorityClasses assign an integer priority value to Pods. When the cluster is resource-constrained, the scheduler will <strong>preempt</strong> (evict) lower-priority Pods to make room for higher-priority ones. System-critical Pods (like CoreDNS) use the built-in <code>system-cluster-critical</code> priority class.</p>" },
      { id: 34, question: "What are Pod Disruption Budgets?", answer: "<p>A PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) defines the minimum number of Pods that must remain available during voluntary disruptions (like node drains, cluster upgrades, or autoscaling). It guarantees that your application maintains quorum during maintenance. Example: <code>minAvailable: 2</code> ensures at least 2 replicas are always running.</p>" },
      { id: 35, question: "What is Pod Topology Spread?", answer: "<p>TopologySpreadConstraints control how Pods are distributed across failure domains (regions, zones, nodes). Example: spreading 10 replicas evenly across 3 AZs (3/3/4 distribution). This is more flexible than anti-affinity rules and supports <code>maxSkew</code> to allow slight imbalances.</p>" },
      { id: 36, question: "How does Kubernetes reschedule Pods after node failure?", answer: "<p>When a node fails: 1) The Node Controller marks the node as <code>NotReady</code>. 2) After the <code>pod-eviction-timeout</code> (default 5 min), Pods on that node are evicted. 3) Controllers (Deployment, ReplicaSet) detect the lost Pods and schedule new ones on healthy nodes. StatefulSet Pods must be manually deleted if the node doesn't recover.</p>" },
      { id: 37, question: "What happens when a node becomes NotReady?", answer: "<p>The Node Controller detects this via the kubelet heartbeat timeout (default 40s). The node is marked <code>NotReady</code> with a taint <code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute</code>. By default, after 300 seconds, Pods are evicted and rescheduled. The node may return to Ready if the issue resolves.</p>" },
      { id: 38, question: "What are Pod lifecycle phases?", answer: "<p>A Pod goes through these phases:</p><ul><li><strong>Pending:</strong> Accepted but not yet scheduled or images not pulled</li><li><strong>Running:</strong> Bound to a node, at least one container is running</li><li><strong>Succeeded:</strong> All containers completed successfully</li><li><strong>Failed:</strong> All containers terminated, at least one failed</li><li><strong>Unknown:</strong> State cannot be determined (node communication issue)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 39, question: "What are Liveness Probes?", answer: "<p>A Liveness Probe tells Kubernetes if a container is alive and healthy. If the probe fails, Kubernetes restarts the container. Used to detect deadlocks or hung processes that haven't crashed but are unresponsive. Probe types: HTTP GET, TCP socket, or exec command.</p>" },
      { id: 40, question: "Difference between Liveness and Readiness probes.", answer: "<p><strong>Liveness:</strong> 'Is the container alive?' — failure triggers a restart.<br><strong>Readiness:</strong> 'Is the container ready to serve traffic?' — failure removes the Pod from Service endpoints (stops receiving traffic) but does NOT restart it.<br><strong>Startup:</strong> Delays liveness checks until the application has started, preventing premature kills of slow-starting apps.</p>" },
      { id: 41, question: "What are Startup Probes?", answer: "<p>Startup Probes disable liveness and readiness probes until the startup probe succeeds. They give slow-starting applications (like JVM apps) time to initialize without being killed by an impatient liveness probe. Once the startup probe succeeds, liveness and readiness probes take over normally.</p>" },
      { id: 42, question: "Explain QoS Classes.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes assigns QoS classes based on resource requests/limits:</p><ul><li><strong>Guaranteed:</strong> requests == limits for all containers (highest priority, evicted last)</li><li><strong>Burstable:</strong> at least one container has requests &lt; limits</li><li><strong>BestEffort:</strong> no requests or limits set (lowest priority, evicted first)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 43, question: "Difference between Guaranteed, Burstable, and BestEffort Pods.", answer: "<p><strong>Guaranteed:</strong> Gets exactly the resources it requests; never throttled; evicted last in memory pressure. <strong>Burstable:</strong> Gets at least its requested amount but can burst; evicted before Guaranteed. <strong>BestEffort:</strong> No resource guarantees; first to be evicted under pressure. For production, always use Guaranteed or Burstable.</p>" },
      { id: 44, question: "How does Kubernetes evict Pods?", answer: "<p>Pod eviction is triggered by resource pressure on a node (disk, memory, CPU) or by the API server during voluntary disruptions. The kubelet evicts BestEffort Pods first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed. PodDisruptionBudgets are respected for voluntary evictions. Involuntary evictions (node failure) ignore PDBs.</p>" },
      { id: 45, question: "Explain graceful termination.", answer: "<p>When a Pod is deleted, Kubernetes sends <code>SIGTERM</code> to the container, giving it <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (default 30s) to shut down gracefully (finish requests, close connections). After the grace period, <code>SIGKILL</code> is sent. A <code>preStop</code> hook can run first to allow load balancer endpoint deregistration before SIGTERM.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "networking",
    title: "3. Kubernetes Networking",
    questions: [
      { id: 46, question: "Explain Kubernetes networking.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes networking is governed by three rules:</p><ol><li>Every Pod gets its own unique IP address</li><li>All Pods can communicate with all other Pods without NAT</li><li>Nodes can communicate with all Pods without NAT</li></ol><p>These rules are implemented by a CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin.</p>" },
      { id: 47, question: "How do Pods communicate?", answer: "<p><strong>Same Node:</strong> Pods communicate via a Linux bridge (cbr0) using their Pod IPs. <strong>Cross-Node:</strong> Traffic is routed through the CNI plugin (overlay network like VXLAN, or underlay like AWS VPC routing). <strong>Via Service:</strong> Traffic hits a ClusterIP, and kube-proxy (iptables/IPVS) rewrites it to a healthy Pod IP.</p>" },
      { id: 48, question: "Explain Service networking.", answer: "<p>When a Service is created, the API Server assigns a ClusterIP from the service CIDR. kube-proxy on each node creates iptables or IPVS rules that intercept traffic to the ClusterIP and DNAT it to a randomly selected Pod IP behind the service. CoreDNS provides DNS resolution for service names.</p>" },
      { id: 49, question: "How does kube-proxy work?", answer: "<p>kube-proxy runs on every node and watches Service and EndpointSlice objects. It programs network rules (iptables or IPVS) to forward traffic destined for a ClusterIP to one of the backing Pod IPs. In iptables mode, rules are evaluated linearly. In IPVS mode, a kernel-level load balancer handles it with better performance at scale.</p>" },
      { id: 50, question: "Difference between iptables and IPVS mode.", answer: "<p><strong>iptables:</strong> Default mode. Uses NAT rules — O(n) lookup time as rules grow. Suffers performance degradation with thousands of services. <strong>IPVS:</strong> Uses Linux Virtual Server — O(1) hash table lookup. Supports multiple load balancing algorithms (RR, Least Connections, IP Hash). Recommended for large clusters (1000+ services).</p>" },
      { id: 51, question: "What is CoreDNS?", answer: "<p>CoreDNS is the DNS server deployed in every Kubernetes cluster. It resolves service names to ClusterIPs. Pods automatically have their <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> configured to point to CoreDNS. Resolution pattern: <code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>. It is extensible via plugins (caching, prometheus metrics, forward to upstream).</p>" },
      { id: 52, question: "How does DNS resolution work?", answer: "<p>When a Pod queries <code>my-service</code>, the resolver appends search domains in order: <code>my-service.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>my-service.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>my-service.cluster.local</code>. CoreDNS returns the ClusterIP. For cross-namespace: use FQDN <code>my-service.other-namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>" },
      { id: 53, question: "What is CNI?", answer: "<p>Container Network Interface (CNI) is a specification for writing plugins that configure network interfaces in containers. When a Pod is created, the kubelet calls the CNI plugin to set up networking. The plugin assigns an IP, creates network interfaces, and sets up routes. Common plugins: AWS VPC CNI, Calico, Cilium, Flannel, Weave.</p>" },
      { id: 54, question: "Explain the AWS VPC CNI plugin.", answer: "<p>AWS VPC CNI assigns native VPC IP addresses directly to Pods (not an overlay). Each Pod IP is a real ENI (Elastic Network Interface) secondary IP on the host EC2 instance. Benefits: no encapsulation overhead, Pod IPs are routable within VPC, integrate with Security Groups for Pods, and VPC Flow Logs capture Pod traffic natively.</p>" },
      { id: 55, question: "What is Prefix Delegation?", answer: "<p>Prefix Delegation improves Pod density on EC2 nodes by assigning /28 IPv4 prefixes to ENIs instead of individual secondary IPs. Each /28 prefix gives 16 IPs, dramatically increasing the number of Pods per node. Reduces IP exhaustion issues on large nodes. Requires Nitro-based EC2 instances.</p>" },
      { id: 56, question: "Explain Custom Networking.", answer: "<p>Custom Networking in AWS VPC CNI allows Pod IPs to come from a different subnet than the node's primary IP. Useful when the primary subnet is exhausted but secondary subnets have IPs available. Pods get IPs from the alternate subnet while the node remains in the primary subnet.</p>" },
      { id: 57, question: "What are Secondary CIDRs?", answer: "<p>Secondary CIDRs are additional IPv4 CIDR blocks attached to a VPC. In EKS, you can add a secondary CIDR (e.g., 100.64.0.0/16, a Carrier Grade NAT range) to provide more IP space for Pods without touching your primary VPC CIDR. Useful when RFC1918 space is limited in large organizations.</p>" },
      { id: 58, question: "What are Security Groups for Pods?", answer: "<p>Security Groups for Pods allows AWS Security Groups to be applied directly to individual Pods (not just nodes). This enables fine-grained, AWS-native network access controls for Pods, allowing them to securely communicate with AWS services or other VPC resources based on SG rules. Requires Nitro instances and specific EKS add-on configuration.</p>" },
      { id: 59, question: "Explain Network Policies.", answer: "<p>NetworkPolicies define firewall rules at the Pod level. Without them, all Pods can communicate freely. With NetworkPolicies, you explicitly allow traffic using ingress/egress rules based on Pod labels, namespaces, or IP CIDRs. They require a supporting CNI plugin (Calico, Cilium, or AWS Network Policy). Default deny-all is a security best practice.</p>" },
      { id: 60, question: "Difference between Calico and AWS Network Policies.", answer: "<p><strong>Calico:</strong> Open-source, supports both NetworkPolicy (standard) and GlobalNetworkPolicy (cluster-wide), uses eBPF or iptables, provides rich egress controls, supports CIDR-based rules. <strong>AWS Network Policy (via VPC CNI):</strong> Uses eBPF, deeply integrated with VPC, lower operational overhead in EKS, but fewer advanced features than Calico.</p>" },
      { id: 61, question: "Explain Ingress Controller.", answer: "<p>An Ingress Controller is the actual implementation (a running deployment) that watches Ingress resources and configures a load balancer accordingly. In AWS EKS, the AWS Load Balancer Controller creates an Application Load Balancer (ALB) for each Ingress resource. NGINX Ingress Controller is a popular self-managed alternative.</p>" },
      { id: 62, question: "Difference between ALB and NLB.", answer: "<p><strong>ALB (Application Load Balancer):</strong> Layer 7, HTTP/HTTPS, supports path/host-based routing, WebSockets, authentication, AWS WAF integration. Ideal for microservices and web apps.<br><strong>NLB (Network Load Balancer):</strong> Layer 4, TCP/UDP/TLS, extremely low latency, handles millions of RPS, preserves client IPs, supports static IPs. Ideal for real-time, high-throughput workloads.</p>" },
      { id: 63, question: "Explain ExternalDNS.", answer: "<p>ExternalDNS is a Kubernetes add-on that automatically manages DNS records in external DNS providers (Route 53, Cloudflare) based on Ingress and Service resources. When you create an Ingress with a hostname annotation, ExternalDNS creates/updates the corresponding DNS record automatically. Eliminates manual DNS management.</p>" },
      { id: 64, question: "How does Route53 integrate with Kubernetes?", answer: "<p>Route 53 integration in EKS is typically done via ExternalDNS. ExternalDNS uses the IAM role (via IRSA) to create/delete/update Route 53 records. When a LoadBalancer Service or Ingress is created with a hostname annotation, ExternalDNS creates an A or CNAME record pointing to the LB endpoint.</p>" },
      { id: 65, question: "Explain Service Mesh.", answer: "<p>A Service Mesh is a dedicated infrastructure layer for service-to-service communication. It intercepts all traffic via sidecar proxies (Envoy) injected into every Pod. Provides: mTLS encryption, traffic management (retries, circuit breaking, canary), and deep observability (metrics, traces per request) without any application code changes.</p>" },
      { id: 66, question: "Istio vs Linkerd.", answer: "<p><strong>Istio:</strong> Feature-rich, complex, uses Envoy proxy, supports advanced traffic management and policy enforcement, large community. Higher resource overhead. <strong>Linkerd:</strong> Simpler, lighter-weight, written in Rust (ultra-low latency proxy), focuses on reliability and observability. Easier to operate but fewer features. Both provide mTLS and observability.</p>" },
      { id: 67, question: "Explain Envoy Proxy.", answer: "<p>Envoy is a high-performance, cloud-native Layer 7 proxy used as the data plane in Istio and other service meshes. It supports gRPC, HTTP/2, dynamic configuration via xDS APIs, rich observability (detailed request metrics/traces), fault injection, retries, circuit breaking, and TLS. Written in C++ for maximum performance.</p>" },
      { id: 68, question: "Explain mTLS.", answer: "<p>Mutual TLS (mTLS) means both the client and server authenticate each other via TLS certificates (unlike standard TLS where only the server authenticates). In a service mesh, every Pod gets an automatically issued certificate (via SPIFFE/SPIRE), ensuring all Pod-to-Pod communication is encrypted and mutually authenticated — zero-trust by default.</p>" },
      { id: 69, question: "Explain East-West traffic.", answer: "<p>East-West traffic refers to communication <em>between</em> services within the cluster (service-to-service). This is the dominant traffic pattern in microservices architectures and is handled by the service mesh or kube-proxy. Security in east-west traffic is enforced via NetworkPolicies and mTLS.</p>" },
      { id: 70, question: "Explain North-South traffic.", answer: "<p>North-South traffic refers to traffic flowing <em>into or out of</em> the cluster — i.e., client requests coming from the internet (Ingress) or services calling external APIs (Egress). In EKS, north-south ingress is handled by ALB/NLB via the AWS Load Balancer Controller, and egress is controlled via NAT Gateway or Egress-only gateways.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "storage",
    title: "4. Storage",
    questions: [
      { id: 71, question: "Explain Persistent Volumes.", answer: "<p>A PersistentVolume (PV) is a cluster-level resource representing a piece of storage (EBS volume, EFS share, NFS mount) provisioned by an admin or dynamically by a StorageClass. It has a lifecycle independent of any individual Pod, persisting data across Pod restarts and rescheduling.</p>" },
      { id: 72, question: "Explain Persistent Volume Claims.", answer: "<p>A PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) is a Pod's request for storage. A Pod declares its storage needs (size, access mode, StorageClass), and Kubernetes binds it to a matching PV. This abstraction decouples Pods from storage implementation details. In EKS, creating a PVC with the <code>gp3-csi</code> StorageClass automatically provisions an EBS volume.</p>" },
      { id: 73, question: "What are Storage Classes?", answer: "<p>StorageClasses define storage 'tiers' and enable dynamic provisioning. Each StorageClass specifies a provisioner (e.g., <code>ebs.csi.aws.com</code>), reclaim policy (<code>Delete</code> or <code>Retain</code>), and parameters (volume type, IOPS, encrypted). When a PVC references a StorageClass, the provisioner automatically creates the backing storage.</p>" },
      { id: 74, question: "Dynamic vs Static provisioning.", answer: "<p><strong>Static:</strong> Admin pre-creates PVs and PVCs bind to them. Manual, predictable but inflexible. <strong>Dynamic:</strong> A StorageClass automates PV creation when a PVC is submitted. The provisioner (EBS CSI, EFS CSI) creates the storage on demand. Dynamic provisioning is the standard approach in cloud environments — no pre-provisioning needed.</p>" },
      { id: 75, question: "Explain EBS CSI Driver.", answer: "<p>The AWS EBS CSI Driver is the official Kubernetes CSI plugin for Amazon Elastic Block Store. It enables dynamic provisioning, attachment, and detachment of EBS volumes to Pods. EBS volumes are <strong>ReadWriteOnce</strong> (single node mount). Supports volume snapshots, volume resizing, and encryption via KMS. Available as an EKS Managed Add-on.</p>" },
      { id: 76, question: "Explain EFS CSI Driver.", answer: "<p>The AWS EFS CSI Driver enables Pods to use Amazon Elastic File System — a fully managed, serverless NFS file system. EFS supports <strong>ReadWriteMany</strong> (many nodes can mount simultaneously). Ideal for shared data between multiple Pods (e.g., CMS uploads, ML training data). Access points provide per-tenant isolation within an EFS file system.</p>" },
      { id: 77, question: "Explain FSx CSI Driver.", answer: "<p>The AWS FSx CSI Driver enables Kubernetes Pods to use Amazon FSx for Lustre (high-performance parallel file system for HPC/ML workloads) or FSx for Windows File Server (SMB/CIFS for Windows workloads). FSx for Lustre integrates with S3 to hydrate data on-demand for large ML training jobs.</p>" },
      { id: 78, question: "Which storage should Stateful applications use?", answer: "<p><strong>Databases (single node):</strong> EBS (gp3) via EBS CSI — high IOPS, low latency, consistent performance.<br><strong>Shared files / CMS:</strong> EFS via EFS CSI — multi-node access, serverless scaling.<br><strong>ML/HPC workloads:</strong> FSx for Lustre — sub-millisecond parallel access.<br>StatefulSets use <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> to provision a dedicated PVC per Pod replica.</p>" },
      { id: 79, question: "Explain Volume Snapshots.", answer: "<p>Volume Snapshots allow point-in-time copies of PersistentVolumes. The EBS CSI Driver supports the VolumeSnapshot API. You create a <code>VolumeSnapshotClass</code>, then a <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> pointing to a PVC. A new PVC can be created from a snapshot for restore or cloning. Essential for database backup strategies in Kubernetes.</p>" },
      { id: 80, question: "Explain CSI architecture.", answer: "<p>CSI (Container Storage Interface) defines a standard API between Kubernetes and storage plugins. Components: <strong>CSI Controller Plugin</strong> (manages volume lifecycle: create, delete, attach, snapshot) runs as a Deployment. <strong>CSI Node Plugin</strong> (mounts/unmounts volumes on nodes) runs as a DaemonSet. Kubernetes communicates via gRPC calls defined in the CSI spec.</p>" },
      { id: 81, question: "Difference between block and file storage.", answer: "<p><strong>Block Storage (EBS):</strong> Low-level, raw storage exposed as a disk to the OS. Best for databases and single-node writes (ReadWriteOnce). Fast, consistent IOPS. <strong>File Storage (EFS, NFS):</strong> Filesystem layer on top, accessible by multiple nodes simultaneously (ReadWriteMany). Best for shared content. Slightly higher latency than block storage.</p>" },
      { id: 82, question: "Explain ephemeral storage.", answer: "<p>Ephemeral storage is temporary storage available to a Pod that is destroyed when the Pod is removed. Types:</p><ul><li><strong>emptyDir:</strong> Created fresh on scheduling; shared between containers in a Pod</li><li><strong>configMap/secret volumes:</strong> Inject configuration files</li><li><strong>Ephemeral inline volumes:</strong> Generic ephemeral volumes managed by CSI drivers (Kubernetes 1.23+)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 83, question: "How does Kubernetes handle storage failures?", answer: "<p>If an EBS volume fails to attach (e.g., due to AZ mismatch or AWS API error), the Pod remains in Pending state. Kubernetes retries the attach operation. If the node itself fails, StatefulSet Pods require manual PVC deletion to trigger rescheduling. Liveness probes can detect application-level storage errors and trigger restarts.</p>" },
      { id: 84, question: "Explain ReadWriteOnce.", answer: "<p><code>ReadWriteOnce (RWO)</code>: The volume can be mounted as read-write by a <em>single node</em> at a time. Most block storage drivers (EBS) support only RWO. Multiple Pods on the <em>same node</em> can use the same RWO volume, but Pods on different nodes cannot.</p>" },
      { id: 85, question: "Explain ReadOnlyMany.", answer: "<p><code>ReadOnlyMany (ROX)</code>: The volume can be mounted as read-only by multiple nodes simultaneously. Useful for distributing static content (configs, models) that should not be modified after creation. EFS, NFS, and some object-store CSI plugins support ROX.</p>" },
      { id: 86, question: "Explain ReadWriteMany.", answer: "<p><code>ReadWriteMany (RWX)</code>: The volume can be mounted as read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously. Required for workloads where multiple Pods on different nodes must read and write the same data (e.g., shared upload directories, ML training). Supported by EFS, NFS, and FSx for Lustre.</p>" },
      { id: 87, question: "How would you migrate Persistent Volumes?", answer: "<p>Common approaches: 1) <strong>Velero:</strong> Backup PVCs as volume snapshots, restore to the target cluster. 2) <strong>AWS EBS Snapshots:</strong> Snapshot the EBS volume, create a new PV from the snapshot in the new cluster. 3) <strong>rsync/rclone:</strong> Copy data from source PVC to target PVC using a migration Pod. 4) <strong>Operator migration:</strong> Use tools like Rook-Ceph migration tools for object storage.</p>" },
      { id: 88, question: "Explain volume expansion.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes supports online volume expansion (without Pod restart) for supported CSI drivers and StorageClasses that have <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>. You simply edit the PVC's <code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> to a larger value. The EBS CSI Driver expands the EBS volume and resizes the filesystem automatically.</p>" },
      { id: 89, question: "Explain StatefulSet storage.", answer: "<p>StatefulSets use <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> to automatically create a unique PVC per Pod replica. Pod <code>my-app-0</code> gets <code>data-my-app-0</code>, Pod <code>my-app-1</code> gets <code>data-my-app-1</code>, etc. When a Pod is rescheduled, it reattaches to its own PVC. Deleting a StatefulSet does NOT delete its PVCs — you must delete them manually.</p>" },
      { id: 90, question: "Best practices for databases in Kubernetes.", answer: "<ul><li>Use StatefulSets with dedicated PVCs per Pod</li><li>Use EBS gp3 volumes with provisioned IOPS for performance-sensitive DBs</li><li>Implement regular snapshot-based backups (Velero or native)</li><li>Set Pod Anti-Affinity to spread replicas across AZs</li><li>Use PodDisruptionBudgets to prevent quorum loss during maintenance</li><li>Consider managed services (RDS, Aurora) for reduced ops overhead</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "security",
    title: "5. Security",
    questions: [
      { id: 91, question: "Explain Kubernetes RBAC.", answer: "<p>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) controls who can perform what actions on which resources. It has four key objects: <strong>Role</strong> (namespace-scoped permissions), <strong>ClusterRole</strong> (cluster-wide permissions), <strong>RoleBinding</strong> (grants a Role to a subject within a namespace), <strong>ClusterRoleBinding</strong> (grants a ClusterRole cluster-wide).</p>" },
      { id: 92, question: "Difference between Role and ClusterRole.", answer: "<p><strong>Role:</strong> Defines permissions within a specific namespace only. Example: allow listing Pods in the 'dev' namespace. <strong>ClusterRole:</strong> Defines permissions cluster-wide, including non-namespaced resources (Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses) or the same permissions across all namespaces. ClusterRoles can be bound with RoleBindings to limit scope to a namespace.</p>" },
      { id: 93, question: "Difference between RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding.", answer: "<p><strong>RoleBinding:</strong> Grants permissions (from a Role or ClusterRole) to subjects within a <em>specific namespace</em>. <strong>ClusterRoleBinding:</strong> Grants permissions from a ClusterRole to subjects across <em>all namespaces</em> or cluster-wide. For least privilege, prefer RoleBindings scoped to individual namespaces.</p>" },
      { id: 94, question: "Explain Service Accounts.", answer: "<p>Service Accounts are Kubernetes identities assigned to Pods, allowing them to authenticate with the Kubernetes API. Every namespace has a default Service Account. Pods use the Service Account's token (mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>) to authenticate API requests. In EKS, Service Accounts are used with IRSA or Pod Identity for AWS IAM permissions.</p>" },
      { id: 95, question: "Explain Pod Identity.", answer: "<p>EKS Pod Identity is AWS's mechanism for granting IAM permissions to Kubernetes Pods without OIDC federation. You associate an IAM role with a Service Account via the EKS Pod Identity Agent (a DaemonSet). The agent injects temporary AWS credentials into Pods. Simpler to configure than IRSA and does not require an OIDC provider per cluster.</p>" },
      { id: 96, question: "Explain IRSA.", answer: "<p>IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) allows EKS Pods to assume IAM roles using OIDC federation. The EKS cluster acts as an OIDC provider. The IAM role trust policy allows tokens from a specific Kubernetes Service Account. Pods assume the role via the AWS SDK's credential chain — no long-lived credentials needed.</p>" },
      { id: 97, question: "Pod Identity vs IRSA.", answer: "<p><strong>IRSA:</strong> Requires OIDC provider setup per cluster, IAM role trust policy with specific OIDC conditions. Works anywhere. <strong>Pod Identity:</strong> Simpler — no OIDC setup; uses an EKS-managed agent; role association created via EKS API. Not yet available for self-managed clusters. Pod Identity is the preferred modern approach for EKS clusters.</p>" },
      { id: 98, question: "Explain IAM authentication in EKS.", answer: "<p>EKS uses AWS IAM for cluster authentication via the <code>aws-auth</code> ConfigMap (legacy) or Access Entries (modern). When you run <code>kubectl</code>, the AWS CLI generates a presigned STS GetCallerIdentity token. The EKS API validates this token and maps the IAM identity to a Kubernetes user/group via the ConfigMap, then RBAC handles authorization.</p>" },
      { id: 99, question: "Explain Kubernetes authentication.", answer: "<p>Authentication methods in Kubernetes:</p><ul><li><strong>X.509 Certificates:</strong> kubectl uses client certs in kubeconfig</li><li><strong>Bearer Tokens:</strong> Service Account JWTs</li><li><strong>OIDC:</strong> Used by EKS for IAM integration</li><li><strong>Webhook Token Auth:</strong> API server calls external webhook to validate tokens</li><li><strong>Proxy Auth:</strong> Authentication proxy in front of API server</li></ul>" },
      { id: 100, question: "Explain Kubernetes authorization.", answer: "<p>After authentication, Kubernetes authorizes requests via:</p><ul><li><strong>RBAC</strong> (most common): Role-based rules</li><li><strong>ABAC:</strong> Attribute-based — file-based policies (deprecated)</li><li><strong>Node Authorization:</strong> Special mode for kubelets</li><li><strong>Webhook:</strong> External service makes authorization decisions</li></ul><p>Multiple authorizers can be chained — any one approval grants access.</p>" },
      { id: 101, question: "Explain admission controllers.", answer: "<p>Admission controllers intercept API server requests <em>after</em> authentication/authorization but <em>before</em> persistence in etcd. Two types:</p><ul><li><strong>Validating:</strong> Accept or reject requests (e.g., reject Pods without resource limits)</li><li><strong>Mutating:</strong> Modify requests (e.g., inject sidecar containers, add default resource limits)</li></ul><p>Implemented as webhooks (OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno).</p>" },
      { id: 102, question: "What is OPA Gatekeeper?", answer: "<p>OPA Gatekeeper is a policy engine that uses Open Policy Agent (OPA) as a Kubernetes admission webhook. Policies are written in <strong>Rego</strong> language and defined as <code>ConstraintTemplate</code> (policy definition) and <code>Constraint</code> (specific instance). Example policies: require resource limits, disallow latest image tags, enforce namespace labels.</p>" },
      { id: 103, question: "What is Kyverno?", answer: "<p>Kyverno is a Kubernetes-native policy engine that uses YAML instead of a custom language (like Rego). Policies can validate, mutate, and generate Kubernetes resources. Easier to adopt than OPA for Kubernetes teams since policies use familiar Kubernetes YAML syntax. Supports policy reports for audit mode.</p>" },
      { id: 104, question: "Explain Pod Security Standards.", answer: "<p>Pod Security Standards (PSS) replaced PodSecurityPolicies (deprecated in 1.21). Three built-in levels enforced via the Pod Security Admission controller:</p><ul><li><strong>Privileged:</strong> No restrictions</li><li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Prevents known privilege escalations</li><li><strong>Restricted:</strong> Hardened, follows pod hardening best practices (no root, read-only FS, etc.)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 105, question: "What is image signing?", answer: "<p>Image signing cryptographically signs container images so consumers can verify the image was built by a trusted source and hasn't been tampered with. Tools: <strong>Cosign</strong> (from Sigstore project) signs images and stores signatures in OCI registries. <strong>Notation</strong> (CNCF). Admission webhooks (Kyverno, Connaisseur) enforce that only signed images run in the cluster.</p>" },
      { id: 106, question: "Explain image scanning.", answer: "<p>Image scanning analyzes container images for known CVEs (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) in OS packages and application dependencies. In AWS: <strong>Amazon ECR Enhanced Scanning</strong> (powered by Inspector) provides continuous scanning. In pipelines: Trivy, Snyk, Grype. Admission controllers can block images with HIGH/CRITICAL severity vulnerabilities from being deployed.</p>" },
      { id: 107, question: "Explain runtime security.", answer: "<p>Runtime security monitors container behavior at runtime to detect anomalies and policy violations after deployment. Unlike static scanning, it catches attacks that happen after a container starts (e.g., unexpected network connections, privilege escalation, process spawning). Tools: Falco, Sysdig Secure, AWS GuardDuty EKS Runtime Monitoring.</p>" },
      { id: 108, question: "Explain Falco.", answer: "<p>Falco is a CNCF open-source runtime security tool that uses Linux kernel eBPF probes to observe system calls. It applies predefined rules (e.g., 'alert if a shell is spawned in a container', 'alert on unexpected outbound connections') and generates security alerts in real-time. Integrates with SIEM systems (Splunk, ELK, CloudWatch).</p>" },
      { id: 109, question: "Explain Secrets Store CSI Driver.", answer: "<p>The Secrets Store CSI Driver mounts secrets from external secret stores (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault) directly into Pods as files. Unlike Kubernetes Secrets, secrets are never stored in etcd. Uses IRSA/Pod Identity to authenticate with AWS Secrets Manager. Supports auto-rotation — Pods get updated secrets without restart.</p>" },
      { id: 110, question: "Explain External Secrets Operator.", answer: "<p>External Secrets Operator (ESO) syncs secrets from external stores (AWS Secrets Manager, SSM Parameter Store, Vault) into standard Kubernetes Secret objects. The operator uses a <code>SecretStore</code> (backend config) and <code>ExternalSecret</code> (what to fetch). Secrets are refreshed on a configurable interval. Uses IRSA for AWS authentication.</p>" },
      { id: 111, question: "Explain AWS Secrets Manager integration.", answer: "<p>Two patterns: 1) <strong>Secrets Store CSI Driver:</strong> Mounts secrets as files from Secrets Manager — no Kubernetes Secret created, more secure. 2) <strong>External Secrets Operator:</strong> Syncs to a Kubernetes Secret — simpler for apps expecting env vars but secret lives in etcd. Both use IRSA/Pod Identity for IAM auth. ESO supports automatic rotation triggers.</p>" },
      { id: 112, question: "Explain KMS encryption.", answer: "<p>AWS Key Management Service (KMS) provides managed encryption keys. In EKS: 1) <strong>Secrets encryption:</strong> KMS encrypts Kubernetes Secrets stored in etcd (envelope encryption). 2) <strong>EBS encryption:</strong> EBS volumes attached to nodes/Pods are encrypted using a KMS CMK. 3) <strong>Application-level:</strong> Apps can use AWS KMS directly via the SDK for field-level encryption.</p>" },
      { id: 113, question: "Explain Envelope Encryption.", answer: "<p>Envelope encryption is a two-tier key hierarchy: 1) A <strong>Data Encryption Key (DEK)</strong> encrypts the actual data. 2) A <strong>Key Encryption Key (KEK)</strong> — stored in KMS — encrypts the DEK. Only the encrypted DEK is stored alongside the data. To decrypt, call KMS to decrypt the DEK, then use the DEK to decrypt the data. This limits exposure of the master key.</p>" },
      { id: 114, question: "Explain Network Policies in Security context.", answer: "<p>NetworkPolicies are the Kubernetes firewall. Security best practices:</p><ul><li>Start with a <strong>default-deny-all</strong> policy in each namespace</li><li>Explicitly allow only required ingress and egress flows</li><li>Use namespace selectors to allow inter-namespace communication</li><li>Block metadata API access: deny egress to 169.254.169.254 for untrusted workloads</li><li>Audit with network policy tools like Kube-Netpol-Analyzer</li></ul>" },
      { id: 115, question: "Explain Zero Trust architecture.", answer: "<p>Zero Trust means 'never trust, always verify' — no implicit trust based on network location. In Kubernetes:</p><ul><li>mTLS between all services (service mesh)</li><li>NetworkPolicies for micro-segmentation</li><li>RBAC with least privilege</li><li>IRSA/Pod Identity (no static credentials)</li><li>Image signing and verification</li><li>Runtime security monitoring</li><li>Audit logging of all API server actions</li></ul>" },
      { id: 116, question: "Explain Supply Chain Security.", answer: "<p>Supply chain security protects the path from source code to running container. Key practices:</p><ul><li><strong>SBOM:</strong> Know all components in your image</li><li><strong>Image signing:</strong> Prove provenance with Cosign</li><li><strong>Image scanning:</strong> Detect CVEs before deployment</li><li><strong>Signed commits:</strong> Verified build pipelines</li><li><strong>Admission policies:</strong> Enforce signed images only</li><li><strong>Build provenance:</strong> SLSA framework attestations</li></ul>" },
      { id: 117, question: "Explain Sigstore.", answer: "<p>Sigstore is an open-source project that provides tools for signing, verifying, and protecting software supply chains. Its components: <strong>Cosign</strong> (signs container images), <strong>Fulcio</strong> (issues short-lived signing certificates using OIDC identity), <strong>Rekor</strong> (immutable, tamper-evident transparency log of all signing events). Enables keyless signing.</p>" },
      { id: 118, question: "Explain Cosign.", answer: "<p>Cosign is a CLI tool from the Sigstore project for signing OCI artifacts (container images, Helm charts). In keyless mode, it uses your OIDC identity (GitHub Actions, Google, AWS) to get a short-lived certificate from Fulcio, signs the image, and records the signature in Rekor. No private key management required. Signatures are stored as OCI artifacts in the same registry.</p>" },
      { id: 119, question: "Explain SBOM.", answer: "<p>A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) is a complete inventory of all components (OS packages, libraries, dependencies) in a software artifact. For containers, it lists everything in the image. Tools: Syft (generates SBOM), Grype (scans SBOM for vulnerabilities). SBOM + Cosign allows you to sign and attest the bill of materials alongside the image signature for supply chain provenance.</p>" },
      { id: 120, question: "Explain Zero Downtime Security Patching.", answer: "<p>To patch nodes without downtime: 1) Enable PodDisruptionBudgets for all critical workloads. 2) Cordon the node (<code>kubectl cordon node</code>) to prevent new Pod scheduling. 3) Drain the node (<code>kubectl drain --ignore-daemonsets</code>) — gracefully evicts Pods respecting PDBs. 4) Apply OS patches. 5) Uncordon the node. With Karpenter, this is automated via drift detection and node replacement.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "eks",
    title: "6. Amazon EKS",
    questions: [
      { id: 121, question: "Explain Amazon EKS architecture.", answer: "<p>EKS provisions a highly available Kubernetes Control Plane managed by AWS across 3 AZs. The Data Plane runs in your VPC. They communicate via EKS-managed ENIs.</p><img src='assets/eks_architecture.png' alt='EKS Architecture' style='width:100%;max-width:600px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;border:1px solid var(--border-color);'>" },
      { id: 122, question: "How does EKS manage the control plane?", answer: "<p>AWS fully manages the control plane: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, and Controller Manager run in an AWS-managed VPC across 3 AZs. AWS handles patching, upgrades, scaling, and HA. You only pay per cluster-hour (~$0.10/hr). You never SSH into control plane nodes. Access is via IAM-authenticated kubectl or AWS API calls.</p>" },
      { id: 123, question: "What are Managed Node Groups?", answer: "<p>Managed Node Groups (MNG) automate EC2 instance provisioning for EKS worker nodes. Features:</p><ul><li>AWS manages the Auto Scaling Group</li><li>Automated rolling updates with graceful draining</li><li>Automatic taint application on unhealthy nodes</li><li>Integration with Karpenter as launch templates</li></ul><p>You choose the instance type, AMI, and scaling bounds.</p>" },
      { id: 124, question: "What are Self-managed Nodes?", answer: "<p>Self-managed nodes are EC2 instances or Auto Scaling Groups you provision and manage yourself. You must manually join them to the cluster (using the bootstrap script in the EKS-optimized AMI), handle OS patching, and manage upgrades. More flexible but significantly higher operational burden than Managed Node Groups.</p>" },
      { id: 125, question: "When would you choose Fargate?", answer: "<p>Use AWS Fargate for EKS when:</p><ul><li>You want serverless compute with no node management</li><li>Strong security isolation is required (each Pod runs in its own microVM)</li><li>Workloads are unpredictable and bursty</li></ul><p>Limitations: no DaemonSets, no GPU support, no privileged containers, higher per-Pod cost vs. EC2 at sustained workloads. Best for stateless web services and batch workloads.</p>" },
      { id: 126, question: "Explain Karpenter.", answer: "<p>Karpenter is an open-source, high-performance cluster autoscaler for Kubernetes. Unlike Cluster Autoscaler (which scales pre-defined node groups), Karpenter watches for unschedulable Pods and directly calls the EC2 API to provision the right node type. It automatically selects instance types that best-fit the Pod requirements and consolidates underutilized nodes.</p>" },
      { id: 127, question: "Explain Cluster Autoscaler.", answer: "<p>Cluster Autoscaler (CAS) monitors Pods that fail to schedule due to insufficient resources and scales up the underlying node group. It also scales down underutilized nodes. CAS is tightly coupled to Cloud Provider Auto Scaling Groups. It requires pre-defined node groups and is slower than Karpenter (typically 2-3 min vs. under 1 min for Karpenter).</p>" },
      { id: 128, question: "Karpenter vs Cluster Autoscaler.", answer: "<table style='width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px'><tr style='background:var(--bg-main)'><th style='padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Feature</th><th style='padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Karpenter</th><th style='padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Cluster Autoscaler</th></tr><tr><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Provisioning speed</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>~30-60s</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>2-3 mins</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Node group dependency</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>None (direct EC2)</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Requires ASG</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Instance selection</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Flexible (any type)</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Pre-configured types</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Bin packing</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Native consolidation</td><td style='padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border-color)'>Limited</td></tr></table>" },
      { id: 129, question: "Explain EKS Auto Mode.", answer: "<p>EKS Auto Mode is a new EKS capability that fully automates compute management. It provisions, configures, and scales EC2 nodes automatically using Karpenter under the hood. It also manages EKS add-ons (VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy) and applies security patches automatically. Designed for teams that want a managed Kubernetes experience with minimal operational overhead.</p>" },
      { id: 130, question: "Explain EKS Pod Identity.", answer: "<p>EKS Pod Identity allows you to assign IAM permissions to Kubernetes Service Accounts without OIDC configuration. The EKS Pod Identity Agent (DaemonSet) intercepts AWS SDK credential requests from Pods and provides temporary credentials via the <code>AssumeRoleForPodIdentity</code> API. Association is created via <code>aws eks create-pod-identity-association</code>.</p>" },
      { id: 131, question: "Explain IRSA in EKS.", answer: "<p>IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts) uses OIDC federation. Steps: 1) Create OIDC provider for your EKS cluster. 2) Create an IAM role with a trust policy referencing the cluster's OIDC provider and specific Service Account. 3) Annotate the Service Account with the role ARN. The AWS SDK automatically exchanges the SA token for temporary AWS credentials via STS.</p>" },
      { id: 132, question: "Explain EKS Add-ons.", answer: "<p>EKS Managed Add-ons are AWS-managed deployments of core cluster components, including:</p><ul><li>Amazon VPC CNI (networking)</li><li>CoreDNS (DNS)</li><li>kube-proxy (service networking)</li><li>AWS EBS CSI Driver (storage)</li><li>Amazon EKS Pod Identity Agent</li><li>AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (observability)</li></ul><p>AWS handles upgrades and compatibility with the cluster version.</p>" },
      { id: 133, question: "Explain AWS Load Balancer Controller.", answer: "<p>The AWS Load Balancer Controller is a Kubernetes controller that provisions AWS load balancers for Kubernetes Services and Ingresses. For <code>Service type=LoadBalancer</code>: provisions an NLB. For <code>Ingress</code>: provisions an ALB with target groups pointing directly to Pod IPs (IP mode) or node ports. Uses IRSA for IAM permissions. Supports many ALB features via annotations.</p>" },
      { id: 134, question: "Explain VPC CNI in EKS.", answer: "<p>The Amazon VPC CNI plugin assigns native VPC IP addresses to Pods. Pods have real ENI secondary IPs — natively routable in the VPC. This enables: direct access to AWS services, Security Groups for Pods, VPC Flow Logs for Pod traffic, and AWS Network Policies. The ipamd daemon pre-warms IPs on each node for fast Pod scheduling.</p>" },
      { id: 135, question: "Explain EKS upgrade strategy.", answer: "<p>EKS upgrades follow this sequence:</p><ol><li>Upgrade the <strong>Control Plane</strong> (one minor version at a time via AWS console/CLI)</li><li>Update <strong>EKS Add-ons</strong> to compatible versions</li><li>Upgrade <strong>Managed Node Groups</strong> (rolling replace with graceful drain)</li></ol><p>Test in staging first. Validate deprecated APIs (use <code>kubectl deprecations</code>). With Karpenter, nodes are replaced via drift detection automatically.</p>" },
      { id: 136, question: "Explain Kubernetes version upgrades.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes follows a n-2 support policy (3 minor versions supported at once). EKS clusters must stay within 2 versions of the latest supported version. Upgrade considerations: API deprecations (run pluto/kubepug), Helm chart compatibility, add-on version alignment, and testing workloads on the new version. Plan for one minor version upgrade at a time.</p>" },
      { id: 137, question: "Explain Blue-Green cluster upgrades.", answer: "<p>Blue-Green cluster upgrades create a completely new 'green' cluster at the target version, migrate workloads, validate, then switch traffic. Benefits: zero-downtime upgrade, easy rollback (just switch back to blue). Steps: 1) Provision new cluster. 2) Deploy all workloads via GitOps. 3) Validate. 4) Switch Route53 / LB traffic. 5) Decommission old cluster. Requires automation (Terraform + ArgoCD).</p>" },
      { id: 138, question: "Explain Multi-AZ architecture.", answer: "<p>For high availability in EKS: deploy nodes across 3 AZs using node groups with subnets in each AZ. Use Pod Anti-Affinity or TopologySpreadConstraints to spread replicas across AZs. Use PodDisruptionBudgets to maintain quorum. For storage, EFS supports multi-AZ access; EBS is single-AZ (use StatefulSet with topology-aware scheduling).</p>" },
      { id: 139, question: "Explain Private EKS clusters.", answer: "<p>A private EKS cluster has both the API server endpoint and nodes in private subnets with no public internet access. Worker nodes communicate with the API server via a private VPC endpoint. Container images must be in ECR or a private registry reachable within VPC. Tools like kubectl run from a bastion host or AWS Systems Manager Session Manager.</p>" },
      { id: 140, question: "Explain Public API Endpoint.", answer: "<p>With public endpoint enabled, the EKS API server is reachable from the internet. Requests from outside AWS travel via the internet to the EKS endpoint. You should restrict access by setting <strong>public access CIDRs</strong> to only your corporate IPs. Public endpoint makes developer access easy but increases attack surface.</p>" },
      { id: 141, question: "Explain Private API Endpoint.", answer: "<p>With private endpoint enabled, the EKS API server is accessible only within your VPC via a VPC endpoint (PrivateLink). All kubectl traffic stays within your VPC/on-prem via Direct Connect or VPN. Recommended for production security. You can enable both public + private endpoints simultaneously with restricted public CIDR allowlists.</p>" },
      { id: 142, question: "Explain Endpoint access configuration.", answer: "<p>EKS endpoint access has three modes: <strong>Public only</strong> (default, internet-accessible), <strong>Public + Private</strong> (nodes use private, external developers use public with CIDR restriction), <strong>Private only</strong> (all access via VPC, most secure). Node-to-API server communication uses the private endpoint regardless of mode.</p>" },
      { id: 143, question: "Explain EKS logging.", answer: "<p>EKS Control Plane logs are published to CloudWatch Logs. Log types: <code>api</code> (all API requests), <code>audit</code> (security audit), <code>authenticator</code> (IAM auth), <code>controllerManager</code>, <code>scheduler</code>. Data Plane logs come from Pods and nodes — use Fluent Bit (DaemonSet) or ADOT to ship to CloudWatch Logs, OpenSearch, or S3.</p>" },
      { id: 144, question: "Explain EKS monitoring.", answer: "<p>Monitoring layers in EKS:</p><ul><li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> CloudWatch Container Insights or Prometheus + Grafana (node CPU/memory/disk)</li><li><strong>Kubernetes objects:</strong> kube-state-metrics (Deployment replicas, Pod status)</li><li><strong>Application:</strong> Custom Prometheus metrics exposed by your apps</li><li><strong>AWS resources:</strong> CloudWatch metrics for ALB, RDS, ElastiCache</li></ul>" },
      { id: 145, question: "Explain EKS backup.", answer: "<p>EKS backup strategies: 1) <strong>Velero:</strong> Backs up Kubernetes resource definitions (YAML) and PVC snapshots to S3. Can restore to a different cluster. 2) <strong>GitOps (ArgoCD/Flux):</strong> Cluster state is in Git — restore by pointing a new cluster at the GitOps repo. 3) <strong>etcd backups:</strong> AWS manages etcd for EKS control plane. 4) <strong>EBS Snapshots:</strong> For stateful data.</p>" },
      { id: 146, question: "Explain Velero.", answer: "<p>Velero is an open-source Kubernetes backup and disaster recovery tool. It can:</p><ul><li>Backup Kubernetes resource definitions to S3</li><li>Take volume snapshots (EBS, EFS) using CSI driver hooks</li><li>Restore to the same or a different cluster</li><li>Schedule recurring backups</li><li>Migrate workloads between clusters or cloud providers</li></ul><p>Uses IRSA for S3 and EBS snapshot permissions.</p>" },
      { id: 147, question: "Explain Multi-account EKS.", answer: "<p>Enterprise EKS deployments often use AWS Organizations with multiple accounts. Patterns: <strong>Hub-and-spoke:</strong> Shared services (ECR, Secrets Manager) in a central account, EKS clusters in workload accounts. Use AWS RAM for cross-account ECR access. Use cross-account IAM roles for tooling. AWS Control Tower enforces guardrails across all accounts.</p>" },
      { id: 148, question: "Explain Multi-cluster EKS.", answer: "<p>Multi-cluster strategies: <strong>Regional clusters:</strong> One cluster per AWS region for data residency and latency. <strong>Environment clusters:</strong> Separate dev/staging/prod clusters. <strong>Workload clusters:</strong> Isolate sensitive workloads (PCI, HIPAA). Management plane: AWS EKS Connector, ArgoCD with ApplicationSets, or Rancher for fleet management.</p>" },
      { id: 149, question: "Explain Hybrid EKS.", answer: "<p>Hybrid EKS allows you to run EKS worker nodes on-premises (bare metal or VMware) while the control plane remains in AWS. Implemented via <strong>EKS Hybrid Nodes</strong> (using SSM or Direct Connect for connectivity) or <strong>AWS Outposts</strong> (fully managed AWS infrastructure in your data center). Useful for data residency requirements or workloads needing on-prem hardware.</p>" },
      { id: 150, question: "Explain EKS Anywhere.", answer: "<p>EKS Anywhere is a deployment option for creating and managing Kubernetes clusters on-premises or in other clouds using the same EKS tooling. It supports bare metal (using Tinkerbell), VMware vSphere, Nutanix, and Snow. You manage the control plane yourself but use the familiar EKS APIs. Connect to AWS via EKS Connector for centralized fleet management.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "scaling",
    title: "7. Scaling & Performance",
    questions: [
      { id: 151, question: "Explain HPA.", answer: "<p>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) automatically scales the number of Pod replicas based on observed metrics. Default: CPU utilization. Advanced: custom metrics (requests/sec, queue depth) via KEDA or Prometheus Adapter. HPA reconciles every 15 seconds and scales between <code>minReplicas</code> and <code>maxReplicas</code>.</p>" },
      { id: 152, question: "Explain VPA.", answer: "<p>Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) automatically adjusts CPU and memory requests/limits for Pods based on historical and current usage. Three modes: <strong>Off</strong> (recommendations only), <strong>Initial</strong> (set only at Pod creation), <strong>Auto</strong> (evict and restart Pods with new settings). Cannot be used with HPA on the same metric.</p>" },
      { id: 153, question: "Explain Cluster Autoscaler in the context of scaling.", answer: "<p>Cluster Autoscaler monitors for pending (unschedulable) Pods and scales up the node group to accommodate them. It also scales down by identifying nodes with low utilization where all Pods could be moved elsewhere. It respects PodDisruptionBudgets during scale-down. Works with AWS Auto Scaling Groups.</p>" },
      { id: 154, question: "Explain Karpenter node provisioning.", answer: "<p>Karpenter watches for unschedulable Pods in real-time and launches exactly the right EC2 instance type within seconds. It evaluates all Pod requirements simultaneously (resource needs, affinity rules, AZ preferences) and selects the most cost-effective instance type. Its consolidation feature actively replaces underutilized nodes with smaller, cheaper alternatives.</p>" },
      { id: 155, question: "Explain node provisioning workflow.", answer: "<p>1. Pod created → kube-scheduler finds no suitable node → Pod marked Pending. 2. Karpenter/CAS detects Pending Pod. 3. Karpenter selects best EC2 instance type → calls EC2 RunInstances API. 4. EC2 instance bootstraps (EKS-optimized AMI runs user-data). 5. kubelet joins cluster. 6. Scheduler places Pod on new node.</p>" },
      { id: 156, question: "Explain CPU throttling.", answer: "<p>CPU throttling occurs when a container exceeds its CPU <em>limit</em>. The Linux CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler) bandwidth control enforces limits — the container is throttled (paused) for the remainder of the scheduling period. Symptoms: high CPU throttling metrics in cAdvisor, increased p99 latency. Solution: increase limits or remove CPU limits for latency-sensitive apps.</p>" },
      { id: 157, question: "Explain memory pressure.", answer: "<p>Memory pressure occurs when a node's available memory is low. The kubelet evicts BestEffort Pods first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed. Hard eviction thresholds (e.g., <code>memory.available &lt; 100Mi</code>) trigger immediate evictions. Soft thresholds trigger evictions with a grace period. OOMKilled events happen when a container exceeds its memory limit.</p>" },
      { id: 158, question: "Explain Pod eviction.", answer: "<p>Pod eviction types: 1) <strong>Resource pressure eviction:</strong> kubelet evicts based on memory/disk pressure. 2) <strong>API-initiated eviction:</strong> <code>kubectl drain</code> or scaling operations. 3) <strong>Preemption:</strong> Scheduler evicts lower-priority Pods for higher-priority ones. PodDisruptionBudgets are respected for voluntary evictions only.</p>" },
      { id: 159, question: "Explain API Server bottlenecks.", answer: "<p>API Server bottlenecks manifest as slow kubectl responses, high API request latency, and watch timeouts. Causes: too many list/watch requests, unindexed informers, large object payloads. Solutions: enable API Priority and Fairness (APF), use server-side apply, use informers with field selectors, separate out high-volume controllers, scale to multi-API-server setups.</p>" },
      { id: 160, question: "Explain etcd performance.", answer: "<p>etcd performance is critical for cluster stability. Slow etcd manifests as slow API server responses. Key metrics: fsync latency (should be &lt;10ms), leader election events, raft proposal failures. Best practices: use fast SSD storage (io1/io2), dedicate etcd nodes, keep etcd database size manageable (compact and defragment regularly), monitor peer latency.</p>" },
      { id: 161, question: "Explain scheduler performance.", answer: "<p>The Kubernetes scheduler can become a bottleneck in large clusters with thousands of Pods. Improvements: use <code>percentageOfNodesToScore</code> to stop scoring after finding enough candidate nodes, use parallel scheduling goroutines, reduce number of priority functions, and avoid overly complex affinity rules. Large clusters may benefit from multiple scheduler instances.</p>" },
      { id: 162, question: "Explain bin packing.", answer: "<p>Bin packing is a scheduling strategy that fills each node to maximum utilization before using new ones, minimizing the number of active nodes. Kubernetes achieves this with the <code>MostAllocated</code> scoring plugin (or Karpenter's consolidation). Benefits: reduced cloud costs. Trade-off: less fault isolation. Use bin packing for batch workloads and spread for HA services.</p>" },
      { id: 163, question: "Explain over-provisioning.", answer: "<p>Over-provisioning maintains spare capacity in the cluster so Pods can be scheduled immediately without waiting for new nodes to spin up. Implemented using low-priority 'placeholder' Pods that consume resources but get evicted when real Pods need to be scheduled. Karpenter's <code>consolidation</code> feature and budgets help balance over-provisioning cost vs. startup latency.</p>" },
      { id: 164, question: "Explain resource requests.", answer: "<p>Resource requests define the <em>minimum</em> CPU and memory the container needs. The scheduler uses requests (not limits) to decide placement. The node's allocatable resources are reserved by the sum of all Pod requests on that node. Always set accurate requests — underestimating causes OOM kills; overestimating wastes capacity and reduces cluster density.</p>" },
      { id: 165, question: "Explain resource limits.", answer: "<p>Resource limits define the <em>maximum</em> CPU and memory a container can use. CPU limits are enforced via throttling (container is paused). Memory limits are enforced via OOM kill (container is killed if it exceeds the limit). Best practice: set memory limits = memory requests (Guaranteed QoS). CPU limits are often omitted for latency-sensitive services to avoid throttling.</p>" },
      { id: 166, question: "Explain QoS tuning.", answer: "<p>QoS tuning strategy: 1) Set <strong>memory requests = limits</strong> for all production Pods (Guaranteed class — safest). 2) For CPU: set requests without limits for latency-sensitive services to avoid throttling. 3) Use VPA in recommendation mode to understand real usage before setting values. 4) Use LimitRanges per namespace to enforce defaults for teams not setting them.</p>" },
      { id: 167, question: "Explain DNS optimization.", answer: "<p>DNS is often a bottleneck in K8s. Optimizations: 1) Set <code>ndots: 2</code> in Pod DNS config to reduce search domain lookups. 2) Use fully qualified domain names (FQDNs) in service calls. 3) Run NodeLocal DNSCache (DaemonSet) to cache DNS on each node and reduce CoreDNS load. 4) Scale CoreDNS horizontally and tune its cache plugin.</p>" },
      { id: 168, question: "Explain container startup optimization.", answer: "<p>Reduce container startup time by: 1) Using distroless or minimal base images. 2) Pre-pulling images to nodes (image warm-up DaemonSet). 3) Using Startup Probes for slow apps. 4) Enabling image layer caching in ECR. 5) Using init containers only when absolutely necessary. 6) Enabling lazy image loading with SOCI Snapshotter or Nydus for large images.</p>" },
      { id: 169, question: "Explain image pull optimization.", answer: "<p>Image pull speed impacts Pod startup time. Strategies: 1) Use <strong>ECR</strong> in the same region as nodes (free and fast). 2) Enable <strong>image pull caching</strong> via containerd's image cache. 3) Use small, layered images (shared base layers don't re-download). 4) Use <strong>AWS SOCI Snapshotter</strong> for lazy loading of large ML model images. 5) Set <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> for stable tags.</p>" },
      { id: 170, question: "Explain large cluster optimization.", answer: "<p>For clusters with 1000+ nodes: 1) Enable <strong>IPVS</strong> mode for kube-proxy. 2) Increase API server request limits and use APF (API Priority and Fairness). 3) Use NodeLocal DNSCache. 4) Use Karpenter instead of CAS. 5) Shard large namespaces. 6) Tune etcd (dedicated SSD, compaction). 7) Use <code>percentageOfNodesToScore</code> in scheduler. 8) Implement hierarchical namespaces for multi-tenancy.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "cicd",
    title: "8. CI/CD & GitOps",
    questions: [
      { id: 171, question: "Explain GitOps.", answer: "<p>GitOps is an operational framework where Git is the single source of truth for declarative infrastructure and application configuration. All changes go through Git (pull requests, reviews, approvals). A GitOps agent (ArgoCD, Flux) continuously reconciles the cluster state to match the Git state. Benefits: audit trail, rollback via revert, drift detection.</p>" },
      { id: 172, question: "Explain ArgoCD.", answer: "<p>ArgoCD is a declarative GitOps continuous delivery tool for Kubernetes. It monitors Git repositories and automatically syncs Kubernetes manifests to the cluster. Key features:</p><ul><li>Visual diff of desired vs actual state</li><li>Automatic or manual sync with health checks</li><li>Rollback to any previous Git commit</li><li>Multi-cluster management from one control plane</li><li>SSO integration (Dex/LDAP)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 173, question: "Explain FluxCD.", answer: "<p>Flux is a CNCF GitOps tool that syncs Kubernetes clusters to Git repositories. Unlike ArgoCD's UI-first approach, Flux is more CLI and API-driven. Key components: Source Controller (watches Git/Helm/OCI repos), Kustomize Controller (applies manifests), Helm Controller (manages Helm releases), Notification Controller (sends alerts). Used natively with Terraform (Flux Terraform Provider).</p>" },
      { id: 174, question: "Helm vs Kustomize.", answer: "<p><strong>Helm:</strong> A package manager with templating. Charts are parameterized with values.yaml. Best for distributing packaged applications (like Prometheus, Nginx). Supports hooks and dependencies. <strong>Kustomize:</strong> Overlay-based configuration management — takes raw YAML and applies patches/overlays per environment (dev/staging/prod). No templating language. Built into kubectl. Better for managing your own configs.</p>" },
      { id: 175, question: "Explain Helm architecture.", answer: "<p>Helm 3 (current) has no server component (Tiller was removed). Components: <strong>Helm CLI:</strong> Client tool that renders templates and talks to the Kubernetes API. <strong>Chart:</strong> Package of Kubernetes YAML templates. <strong>Release:</strong> A deployed instance of a chart. <strong>Repository:</strong> HTTP server hosting chart archives (ECR supports OCI chart storage). Release history stored in Kubernetes Secrets.</p>" },
      { id: 176, question: "Explain Helm Charts.", answer: "<p>A Helm Chart is a collection of files: <code>Chart.yaml</code> (metadata), <code>values.yaml</code> (default values), <code>templates/</code> (Go template YAML files), <code>charts/</code> (sub-charts/dependencies). Charts are versioned, testable (<code>helm test</code>), and support lifecycle hooks. Packaged as <code>.tgz</code> archives and stored in Helm repositories (OCI registries, ChartMuseum).</p>" },
      { id: 177, question: "Explain Helm Hooks.", answer: "<p>Helm Hooks allow actions at specific lifecycle events: <code>pre-install</code>, <code>post-install</code>, <code>pre-upgrade</code>, <code>post-upgrade</code>, <code>pre-delete</code>, <code>post-delete</code>, <code>test</code>. Hooks are Kubernetes Jobs or Pods with a <code>helm.sh/hook</code> annotation. Use cases: run database migrations before upgrade, send Slack notifications post-deploy, run integration tests post-install.</p>" },
      { id: 178, question: "Explain Progressive Delivery.", answer: "<p>Progressive Delivery is an evolution of CI/CD that gradually shifts traffic to new versions, using automated analysis to decide whether to proceed or rollback. Implementations: <strong>Canary releases</strong> (small % of traffic), <strong>Feature flags</strong>, <strong>Blue-Green deployments</strong>. Tools: Argo Rollouts, Flagger (automated canary with Prometheus metrics analysis).</p>" },
      { id: 179, question: "Explain Blue-Green deployment.", answer: "<p>Blue-Green deployment maintains two identical production environments (Blue = current, Green = new). Traffic is fully switched from Blue to Green via a load balancer or Route53 weighted routing. Both environments run simultaneously during the switch, enabling instant rollback. Cost: 2x infrastructure. Best for: major version changes requiring quick rollback capability.</p>" },
      { id: 180, question: "Explain Canary deployment.", answer: "<p>Canary deployment gradually shifts a percentage of traffic to the new version while monitoring for errors. Example: 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%. If error rate exceeds threshold, auto-rollback. In Kubernetes: use Argo Rollouts with Prometheus analysis templates or Istio traffic weights. Reduces blast radius of bad deployments.</p>" },
      { id: 181, question: "Explain Rolling Update.", answer: "<p>Rolling Update is Kubernetes Deployment's default strategy. It gradually replaces old Pods with new ones. Parameters:</p><ul><li><code>maxUnavailable</code>: How many old Pods can be unavailable at once</li><li><code>maxSurge</code>: How many extra Pods can run above desired count</li></ul><p>Ensures zero downtime. Rollback with <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app</code>.</p>" },
      { id: 182, question: "Explain Rollback strategy.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes keeps a configurable history of ReplicaSet revisions (default 10). To rollback: <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app</code> (reverts to previous). With ArgoCD: sync to a specific Git commit SHA. With Helm: <code>helm rollback my-release 3</code> (roll back to revision 3). For Blue-Green: switch traffic back to Blue immediately.</p>" },
      { id: 183, question: "Explain GitHub Actions with Kubernetes.", answer: "<p>Typical GitHub Actions → Kubernetes pipeline: 1) Code push triggers workflow. 2) Build Docker image and push to ECR. 3) Run tests, security scans (Trivy). 4) Sign image with Cosign. 5) Update image tag in GitOps repo (Helm values or Kustomize overlay). 6) ArgoCD/Flux detects the Git change and syncs to the cluster. No direct kubectl in CI — use GitOps.</p>" },
      { id: 184, question: "Explain Jenkins with Kubernetes.", answer: "<p>Jenkins runs as a Deployment in Kubernetes. The Kubernetes plugin dynamically provisions Pod-based agents for each build job, using templates to define build containers. Build agents are ephemeral — created for the job, destroyed on completion. Persistent data (artifacts, cache) stored in PVCs. Scales automatically with cluster autoscaling.</p>" },
      { id: 185, question: "Explain Image promotion.", answer: "<p>Image promotion is the process of moving a container image through environments (dev → staging → prod) using the same immutable image digest. Workflow: CI builds image tagged with git SHA → promoted to staging → validated → tag promoted to prod. Never rebuild — promote the exact image that was tested. Ensures reproducibility across environments.</p>" },
      { id: 186, question: "Explain Git branching strategy.", answer: "<p>Common strategies for GitOps: <strong>Environment branches:</strong> <code>main</code> = prod, <code>staging</code> = staging — changes flow through merges. Simple but can diverge. <strong>Directory-based:</strong> One branch, separate directories for each environment. Used with Kustomize overlays. <strong>Trunk-based with tags:</strong> Single branch, environment promotion via image tags. Recommended for most teams.</p>" },
      { id: 187, question: "Explain Multi-environment deployment.", answer: "<p>Multi-environment strategy with GitOps: One repo per environment or one repo with environment directories. ArgoCD ApplicationSets can deploy the same app to multiple clusters/namespaces with different values. Kustomize base/overlay pattern: <code>base/</code> has common manifests, <code>overlays/dev/</code>, <code>overlays/prod/</code> patch environment-specific values (replicas, resource limits, hostnames).</p>" },
      { id: 188, question: "Explain Secret management in GitOps.", answer: "<p>Secrets should never be in Git plain text. Solutions:</p><ul><li><strong>Sealed Secrets:</strong> Encrypt secrets with cluster-specific public key — encrypted secret is safe to commit</li><li><strong>External Secrets Operator:</strong> Reference secrets in AWS Secrets Manager from Git — no secret values in Git</li><li><strong>SOPS:</strong> Encrypt secrets in Git files using KMS — decrypted by ArgoCD/Flux using IRSA</li></ul>" },
      { id: 189, question: "Explain Drift detection.", answer: "<p>Drift is when the cluster's actual state diverges from the desired state in Git (e.g., someone runs <code>kubectl edit</code> manually). ArgoCD detects drift on every sync cycle and shows the resource as OutOfSync. It can be configured to auto-heal (re-apply Git state) or alert on drift. Drift detection prevents snowflake clusters and enforces GitOps discipline.</p>" },
      { id: 190, question: "Explain GitOps reconciliation.", answer: "<p>GitOps reconciliation is the continuous loop where the agent (ArgoCD/Flux): 1) Polls Git repo for changes (or receives webhook). 2) Renders manifests (Helm template / Kustomize build). 3) Compares rendered manifests with live cluster state. 4) Applies differences if auto-sync is enabled or waits for manual approval. Default ArgoCD sync interval: 3 minutes.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "monitoring",
    title: "9. Monitoring & Logging",
    questions: [
      { id: 191, question: "Explain Prometheus.", answer: "<p>Prometheus is an open-source time-series database and monitoring system. It scrapes metrics from instrumented targets via HTTP. Key components: Prometheus Server (scrapes, stores, evaluates rules), Alertmanager (handles alerts), Pushgateway (for short-lived jobs). Kubernetes-native with service discovery via <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CRDs (via kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart).</p>" },
      { id: 192, question: "Explain Alertmanager.", answer: "<p>Alertmanager handles alerts sent by Prometheus. It provides: <strong>Grouping</strong> (batches related alerts), <strong>Inhibition</strong> (silences certain alerts when others fire), <strong>Silencing</strong> (mute during maintenance), <strong>Routing</strong> (sends alerts to PagerDuty, Slack, email based on labels). Alert rules are defined in Prometheus using PromQL.</p>" },
      { id: 193, question: "Explain Grafana.", answer: "<p>Grafana is an open-source analytics and visualization platform. It connects to data sources (Prometheus, CloudWatch, Loki, Elasticsearch) and provides dashboards. Key features: alerting, annotations, templating (dashboard variables), provisioning (dashboards as code). Amazon Managed Grafana eliminates operational overhead of running Grafana yourself.</p>" },
      { id: 194, question: "Explain Amazon Managed Prometheus.", answer: "<p>Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus (AMP) is a serverless, Prometheus-compatible monitoring service. It auto-scales storage and query capacity without managing a Prometheus server. EKS clusters send metrics via the Prometheus remote_write protocol or ADOT Collector. Compatible with all PromQL queries and Grafana dashboards. Integrates with AWS PrivateLink for secure data ingestion.</p>" },
      { id: 195, question: "Explain Amazon Managed Grafana.", answer: "<p>Amazon Managed Grafana (AMG) is a fully managed Grafana service. Eliminates server management, high availability setup, and upgrades. Integrates natively with AWS IAM for authentication, supports data sources like AMP, CloudWatch, X-Ray, and Athena. Enables enterprise features (SAML SSO, LDAP) without Grafana Enterprise licensing costs.</p>" },
      { id: 196, question: "Explain OpenTelemetry.", answer: "<p>OpenTelemetry (OTel) is a CNCF standard for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data (metrics, logs, traces). It provides vendor-neutral SDKs and the OTel Collector (a data pipeline agent). In Kubernetes: auto-instrument pods with OTel sidecars or operators, collect data via the Collector, export to backends (Jaeger, Prometheus, X-Ray, Grafana Tempo).</p>" },
      { id: 197, question: "Explain ADOT.", answer: "<p>AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) is AWS's supported, production-ready distribution of OpenTelemetry. It provides: the ADOT Collector (pre-configured to send to AWS backends), auto-instrumentation for Java/Python/Node, and integration with X-Ray and AMP. Available as an EKS Managed Add-on. Enables standardized observability for AWS environments.</p>" },
      { id: 198, question: "Explain Fluent Bit.", answer: "<p>Fluent Bit is a lightweight, high-performance log processor and forwarder. In EKS, it runs as a DaemonSet to collect logs from all containers (via <code>/var/log/containers</code>) and nodes, then ships them to CloudWatch Logs, OpenSearch, Kinesis, or S3. Lower resource overhead than Fluentd (~450KB vs ~40MB memory). AWS provides a pre-configured Fluent Bit image for CloudWatch Logs.</p>" },
      { id: 199, question: "Explain Fluentd.", answer: "<p>Fluentd is an open-source data collector for unified logging. More mature than Fluent Bit with a richer plugin ecosystem (500+ plugins). Used when complex log routing, transformation, or filtering is needed. Higher resource usage than Fluent Bit. In large clusters, use Fluentd as an aggregator and Fluent Bit as a lightweight forwarder on each node.</p>" },
      { id: 200, question: "Explain Loki.", answer: "<p>Grafana Loki is a horizontally scalable, highly available log aggregation system inspired by Prometheus. Unlike Elasticsearch, Loki indexes only log metadata (labels), not the full log content — making it cost-efficient. Logs are stored in S3. Query with LogQL (similar to PromQL). Pairs with Grafana for visualization and Promtail/Fluent Bit for log collection.</p>" },
      { id: 201, question: "Explain ELK.", answer: "<p>ELK Stack: <strong>Elasticsearch</strong> (distributed search engine for log storage), <strong>Logstash</strong> (log processing pipeline), <strong>Kibana</strong> (visualization UI). In AWS: managed via Amazon OpenSearch Service. Rich full-text search capabilities. Higher cost and complexity than Loki. Best for use cases requiring complex log queries, correlation, and compliance reporting.</p>" },
      { id: 202, question: "Explain CloudWatch Container Insights.", answer: "<p>CloudWatch Container Insights collects, aggregates, and summarizes metrics and logs from containerized applications on EKS and ECS. It provides pre-built dashboards for: cluster health, node performance, Pod CPU/memory, service error rates. Uses the ADOT Collector or CloudWatch Agent DaemonSet. Integrates with CloudWatch Alarms for automated responses.</p>" },
      { id: 203, question: "Explain distributed tracing.", answer: "<p>Distributed tracing tracks a single request as it flows through multiple microservices, showing exactly where latency occurs. Each service adds trace context (Trace ID, Span ID) to requests. A trace is composed of spans — each span represents one operation. Tools: Jaeger, Zipkin, AWS X-Ray, Grafana Tempo. Essential for diagnosing latency in microservices architectures.</p>" },
      { id: 204, question: "Explain Jaeger.", answer: "<p>Jaeger is a CNCF open-source, end-to-end distributed tracing system. It collects, stores, and visualizes traces from instrumented services. Compatible with OpenTelemetry. Deployable in Kubernetes via the Jaeger Operator. Storage backends: Elasticsearch, Cassandra, or in-memory (dev). Enables identification of performance bottlenecks across service calls.</p>" },
      { id: 205, question: "Explain X-Ray.", answer: "<p>AWS X-Ray is a managed distributed tracing service. It traces requests through AWS services (API Gateway, Lambda, ECS, EKS, RDS) and custom services instrumented with the X-Ray SDK or OpenTelemetry (via ADOT). Provides Service Map visualization, latency histograms, and annotation-based filtering. No infrastructure to manage. Integrates with CloudWatch.</p>" },
      { id: 206, question: "Explain metrics vs logs vs traces.", answer: "<p><strong>Metrics:</strong> Aggregated numerical measurements over time (CPU %, request rate, error count). Low storage cost, good for alerting and dashboards. <strong>Logs:</strong> Time-stamped text records of discrete events. High storage cost, good for debugging specific incidents. <strong>Traces:</strong> Correlated sequence of events across services for a single request. Best for understanding latency and request flow. Use all three together (observability trinity).</p>" },
      { id: 207, question: "Explain RED metrics.", answer: "<p>RED is a methodology for monitoring microservices:</p><ul><li><strong>Rate:</strong> Requests per second — how busy is the service?</li><li><strong>Errors:</strong> Error rate — how many requests are failing?</li><li><strong>Duration:</strong> Latency distribution (p50, p95, p99) — how long do requests take?</li></ul><p>RED focuses on the user experience of a service, making it ideal for SLI/SLO definitions.</p>" },
      { id: 208, question: "Explain USE metrics.", answer: "<p>USE is a methodology for monitoring infrastructure resources:</p><ul><li><strong>Utilization:</strong> Percentage of time the resource is busy (CPU %, disk I/O %)</li><li><strong>Saturation:</strong> Amount of work the resource cannot service yet (run queue length, memory swap)</li><li><strong>Errors:</strong> Count of error events (network packet drops, disk errors)</li></ul><p>USE focuses on resource health, complementing RED's service focus.</p>" },
      { id: 209, question: "Explain SLI, SLO, SLA.", answer: "<p><strong>SLI (Service Level Indicator):</strong> A quantitative measure of service behavior — the actual metric (e.g., 99.5% of requests completed in &lt;200ms this month). <strong>SLO (Service Level Objective):</strong> The target you aim to meet (e.g., 99.9% availability). <strong>SLA (Service Level Agreement):</strong> A contractual commitment with consequences for breach (e.g., 99.5% uptime or refund). SLO should be more ambitious than SLA.</p>" },
      { id: 210, question: "Explain observability architecture.", answer: "<p>A complete observability stack for EKS: <strong>Metrics:</strong> Prometheus (scraping) → AMP (storage) → AMG (visualization). <strong>Logs:</strong> Fluent Bit DaemonSet → CloudWatch Logs or Loki + S3. <strong>Traces:</strong> ADOT Collector → X-Ray or Jaeger. <strong>Alerting:</strong> Alertmanager → PagerDuty/Slack. <strong>SLO tracking:</strong> Sloth or OpenSLO for SLO dashboards. All integrated with GitOps for config-as-code.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "troubleshooting",
    title: "10. Troubleshooting",
    questions: [
      { id: 211, question: "A Pod is stuck in Pending. How do you troubleshoot?", answer: "<p><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> — look at Events section. Common causes:</p><ul><li><strong>Insufficient resources:</strong> Node doesn't have enough CPU/memory → scale up cluster</li><li><strong>NodeSelector/Affinity mismatch:</strong> No node matches labels → fix selector or add label to node</li><li><strong>Taint not tolerated:</strong> Add matching toleration to Pod</li><li><strong>PVC Pending:</strong> StorageClass provisioning failure</li><li><strong>No nodes available:</strong> All nodes cordoned/tainted</li></ul>" },
      { id: 212, question: "A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. What do you check?", answer: "<p>CrashLoopBackOff means the container keeps crashing and Kubernetes keeps restarting it. Debug steps:</p><ol><li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — logs from the crashed container</li><li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> — check exit codes and events</li><li>Check for OOMKilled (exit code 137) → increase memory limit</li><li>Check for misconfiguration (bad env vars, wrong config file)</li><li>Check readiness/liveness probe — misconfigured probe kills healthy container</li></ol>" },
      { id: 213, question: "ImagePullBackOff error troubleshooting.", answer: "<p>ImagePullBackOff means Kubernetes can't pull the container image. Checklist:</p><ul><li>Image name/tag typo — verify with <code>docker pull</code></li><li>Private registry credentials — check <code>imagePullSecrets</code> is configured</li><li>ECR authentication — check node IAM role has <code>ecr:GetAuthorizationToken</code> permission</li><li>Image doesn't exist — verify in ECR console</li><li>Node can't reach registry — check security groups/network ACLs</li></ul>" },
      { id: 214, question: "OOMKilled troubleshooting.", answer: "<p>OOMKilled (exit code 137) means the container exceeded its memory limit. Steps:</p><ol><li>Check metrics: <code>kubectl top pod &lt;pod&gt;</code></li><li>Review memory limit — compare with actual peak usage</li><li>Look for memory leak in application logs</li><li>Increase memory limit if usage is legitimate</li><li>Set Guaranteed QoS (requests == limits) to avoid unexpected eviction</li><li>Enable JVM heap monitoring for Java applications</li></ol>" },
      { id: 215, question: "ContainerCreating state troubleshooting.", answer: "<p>Pod stuck in ContainerCreating means the container hasn't started yet. <code>kubectl describe pod</code> shows the reason:</p><ul><li><strong>Volume mount failure:</strong> PVC not bound, wrong volume name</li><li><strong>Image pulling:</strong> Large image still downloading</li><li><strong>ConfigMap/Secret not found:</strong> Referenced resource doesn't exist</li><li><strong>Init container running:</strong> Waiting for init containers to complete</li><li><strong>Node disk pressure:</strong> Node out of ephemeral storage</li></ul>" },
      { id: 216, question: "Pods cannot resolve DNS.", answer: "<p>DNS resolution issues in Kubernetes:</p><ol><li>Check if CoreDNS Pods are Running: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></li><li>Test from inside Pod: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- nslookup kubernetes.default</code></li><li>Check CoreDNS logs for errors</li><li>Verify <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> inside Pod points to CoreDNS ClusterIP</li><li>Check NetworkPolicy isn't blocking UDP 53 to CoreDNS</li><li>Check ndots setting causing excessive search domain queries</li></ol>" },
      { id: 217, question: "ALB returns 502.", answer: "<p>502 Bad Gateway from ALB means the target (Pod) returned an invalid response. Checklist:</p><ul><li>Pod is Running and Healthy — check <code>kubectl get pods</code></li><li>Target group health — check in AWS console (unhealthy targets)</li><li>Readiness probe — Pod marked ready but app isn't responding</li><li>App crashing on startup — check container logs</li><li>Security group — ALB security group can reach Pod port</li><li>Wrong target port in Ingress annotation</li></ul>" },
      { id: 218, question: "ALB returns 504.", answer: "<p>504 Gateway Timeout means the target didn't respond in time. Checklist:</p><ul><li>Application processing too slow — check app performance and DB query times</li><li>ALB idle timeout (default 60s) vs app response time — increase if needed</li><li>Long-polling or streaming endpoints — ensure keep-alive is configured</li><li>Pod startup too slow — check if requests hitting pods before ready</li><li>Database connection exhaustion — check DB connection pool metrics</li></ul>" },
      { id: 219, question: "Node is NotReady.", answer: "<p>Troubleshooting a NotReady node:</p><ol><li><code>kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt;</code> — check Conditions and Events</li><li>SSH to node (via SSM): check kubelet status — <code>systemctl status kubelet</code></li><li>Check kubelet logs: <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code></li><li>Common causes: disk pressure, memory pressure, network partition, kubelet crash</li><li>Check node security group allows communication with API server</li><li>Verify instance is healthy in EC2 console</li></ol>" },
      { id: 220, question: "etcd latency issues.", answer: "<p>High etcd latency causes slow API server responses. Diagnosis:</p><ul><li>Check etcd metrics: <code>etcd_disk_wal_fsync_duration_seconds</code></li><li>Look for disk I/O saturation on etcd nodes</li><li>Check network latency between etcd peers</li><li>Run <code>etcdctl defrag</code> to reclaim space (schedule during maintenance)</li><li>In EKS: AWS manages etcd — open a support ticket if API latency is high</li></ul>" },
      { id: 221, question: "API Server is slow.", answer: "<p>Slow API server symptoms: kubectl commands time out, Prometheus scraping fails, HPA stops working. Causes:</p><ul><li>Too many list/watch requests from controllers</li><li>etcd latency (see above)</li><li>API Priority and Fairness (APF) exhausted</li><li>Large resource objects causing slow serialization</li></ul><p>In EKS: check API server metrics in CloudWatch. Use <code>--request-timeout</code> in kubectl. Enable API server audit logs to identify hot paths.</p>" },
      { id: 222, question: "Worker node unreachable.", answer: "<p>Checklist: 1) EC2 instance running in AWS console? 2) Node security group allows inbound from API server (port 443/10250)? 3) Is the node's IAM role present? 4) kubelet running on the node (check via SSM)? 5) Network connectivity — route tables, VPC endpoints. 6) Check if AWS Systems Manager Agent is running for SSM access. 7) VPC DHCP options correct?</p>" },
      { id: 223, question: "PVC Pending.", answer: "<p>PVC stuck in Pending means no PV matched or dynamic provisioning failed:</p><ul><li><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;pvc&gt;</code> — look at Events</li><li>StorageClass mismatch — PVC requests a StorageClass that doesn't exist</li><li>Access mode mismatch — PVC requests RWX but StorageClass only supports RWO</li><li>CSI driver not installed — EBS/EFS CSI driver pod not running</li><li>IAM permission — node role missing EBS/EFS permissions</li><li>AZ mismatch — EBS volume in different AZ than node</li></ul>" },
      { id: 224, question: "EBS volume not attaching.", answer: "<p>EBS volume attachment failures:</p><ul><li>AZ mismatch: Pod scheduled in us-east-1a but EBS volume is in us-east-1b — use topology-aware scheduling</li><li>Volume limit: each EC2 instance has a maximum EBS volume attachment limit</li><li>IAM permission: node role missing <code>ec2:AttachVolume</code></li><li>CSI driver pod crash — <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app=ebs-csi-node</code></li><li>Multi-attach: another Pod still holding the volume — StatefulSet Pod stuck</li></ul>" },
      { id: 225, question: "Ingress not routing traffic.", answer: "<p>Debug steps for Ingress routing failures:</p><ol><li>Check Ingress resource: <code>kubectl describe ingress &lt;name&gt;</code></li><li>Verify ALB is created in AWS console</li><li>Check ALB target group health (are Pods registered and healthy?)</li><li>Verify Service selects the right Pods (correct label selector)</li><li>Check ALB security group allows port 80/443 from internet</li><li>Verify annotations on Ingress (e.g., <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code>)</li><li>Check AWS LBC controller logs</li></ol>" },
      { id: 226, question: "NetworkPolicy blocking traffic.", answer: "<p>To diagnose NetworkPolicy issues:</p><ul><li>Check all NetworkPolicies in the namespace: <code>kubectl get networkpolicies</code></li><li>Test connectivity: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- curl &lt;target&gt;</code></li><li>Use <code>kubectl-netpol</code> or Cilium NetworkPolicy editor to visualize rules</li><li>Temporarily delete all NetworkPolicies to confirm they're the cause</li><li>Remember: policies are additive — if any policy selects a Pod, all non-matching traffic is denied</li></ul>" },
      { id: 227, question: "Service not reachable.", answer: "<p>Debugging Service unreachability:</p><ol><li>Verify Service endpoint: <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — should list Pod IPs</li><li>If endpoints empty: Pod label doesn't match Service selector</li><li>Test ClusterIP directly from another Pod</li><li>Check kube-proxy rules: <code>iptables -t nat -L | grep &lt;clusterip&gt;</code></li><li>Check Pod is Running and passing readiness probe</li><li>Verify port numbers match (Service port vs container port)</li></ol>" },
      { id: 228, question: "Pod-to-Pod communication failing.", answer: "<p>Diagnosis:</p><ol><li>Ping Pod IP directly: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- ping &lt;target-pod-ip&gt;</code></li><li>Check NetworkPolicies blocking traffic</li><li>Verify CNI plugin is functioning: check VPC CNI logs on source node</li><li>Check node security groups allow traffic between nodes (for cross-node communication)</li><li>Verify pods are in overlapping IP CIDR ranges</li><li>Check VPC route tables for pod CIDR routes</li></ol>" },
      { id: 229, question: "High CPU on worker nodes.", answer: "<p>Investigation steps:</p><ul><li><code>kubectl top nodes</code> and <code>kubectl top pods</code> — identify which Pod is consuming CPU</li><li>Check for CPU limits causing throttling vs actual high usage</li><li>Profile the application (Java: async-profiler, Go: pprof endpoint)</li><li>Check for noisy neighbor effect — other Pods on the same node</li><li>Look for infinite loops in application code</li><li>Consider moving CPU-intensive workloads to dedicated nodes with taints</li></ul>" },
      { id: 230, question: "High memory usage.", answer: "<p>Debugging high memory:</p><ul><li><code>kubectl top pods</code> — identify high consumers</li><li>Check for memory leaks — memory climbing continuously without releasing</li><li>Review JVM heap settings for Java apps (often set too high)</li><li>Check for large in-memory caches</li><li>Use memory profiling tools (pprof for Go, jmap for Java)</li><li>Set appropriate memory limits and OOMKill threshold alerts</li><li>Consider using VPA recommendations for right-sizing</li></ul>" },
      { id: 231, question: "Container restart loops.", answer: "<p>Container restart loop root causes:</p><ul><li><strong>OOMKilled:</strong> Increase memory limit or fix memory leak</li><li><strong>Liveness probe failure:</strong> App not responding within probe timeout — fix probe or tune thresholds</li><li><strong>Application exception on startup:</strong> Check logs with <code>--previous</code> flag</li><li><strong>Missing secret/configmap:</strong> App fails to start without required config</li><li><strong>Port conflict:</strong> App trying to bind port already in use</li></ul>" },
      { id: 232, question: "Certificate expiration.", answer: "<p>Kubernetes certificates expire and cause auth failures. In EKS: AWS manages control plane certs automatically. For in-cluster components (webhooks, custom CAs): use cert-manager to automate certificate lifecycle. Alert on expiry with <code>certmanager_certificate_expiration_timestamp_seconds</code>. For node certificates (kubelet): managed by EKS when you use Managed Node Groups. Rotate via node replacement.</p>" },
      { id: 233, question: "Cluster upgrade failure.", answer: "<p>Cluster upgrade failure recovery:</p><ol><li>EKS control plane upgrades are managed — AWS provides rollback if upgrade fails</li><li>Data plane upgrade failure: node fails to join after kubelet upgrade — check bootstrap logs via SSM</li><li>Deprecated API usage causing webhook failures — run <code>pluto</code> to detect deprecated APIs pre-upgrade</li><li>Add-on incompatibility — always upgrade add-ons after control plane</li><li>Re-run upgrade with verbose logging enabled</li></ol>" },
      { id: 234, question: "Scheduler not scheduling Pods.", answer: "<p>When kube-scheduler stops scheduling Pods:</p><ul><li>Verify scheduler is running: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler</code></li><li>In EKS: scheduler is managed by AWS — check AWS Status page</li><li>Check for API server connectivity issues (scheduler can't reach API server)</li><li>Verify no resource quotas are blocking — <code>kubectl describe quota</code></li><li>Check for PriorityClass exhaustion preventing lower-priority Pods</li></ul>" },
      { id: 235, question: "Control Plane connectivity issues.", answer: "<p>EKS control plane connectivity troubleshooting:</p><ol><li>Test API connectivity: <code>kubectl cluster-info</code></li><li>Check EKS endpoint access settings (public/private)</li><li>Verify VPC endpoint for EKS if using private endpoint</li><li>Check security groups on nodes — outbound 443 to API server endpoint</li><li>Verify IAM permissions — ensure the calling identity is in aws-auth or Access Entries</li><li>Check Route53 private hosted zone if using private endpoint</li></ol>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "architecture",
    title: "11. Architecture & Design",
    questions: [
      { id: 236, question: "Design an EKS platform for 500+ microservices.", answer: "<p><strong>Platform layers:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Compute:</strong> Karpenter with mixed Spot/On-Demand. Separate node pools for critical/batch workloads.</li><li><strong>Networking:</strong> AWS Load Balancer Controller + ExternalDNS + Istio service mesh</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> EBS CSI (stateful) + EFS CSI (shared)</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> OPA Gatekeeper policies, IRSA/Pod Identity, Secrets Store CSI Driver</li><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> ArgoCD with ApplicationSets for multi-service GitOps</li><li><strong>Observability:</strong> AMP + AMG + X-Ray + Fluent Bit → CloudWatch</li><li><strong>Multi-tenancy:</strong> Namespaces per team with ResourceQuotas and RBAC</li></ul>" },
      { id: 237, question: "How would you design a multi-tenant Kubernetes platform?", answer: "<p>Multi-tenancy patterns:</p><ul><li><strong>Soft multi-tenancy:</strong> Namespace per tenant, RBAC, ResourceQuotas, NetworkPolicies for isolation. Simpler but shared kernel.</li><li><strong>Hard multi-tenancy:</strong> Separate clusters per tenant (or Fargate for maximum isolation). Higher cost.</li><li><strong>Hierarchical Namespaces:</strong> HNC operator enables namespace hierarchies with inherited policies.</li><li><strong>vClusters:</strong> Virtual clusters running within namespaces — strong isolation with shared infrastructure.</li></ul><p>For enterprises: combine namespace isolation + Kyverno policies + separate node pools with taints per tenant.</p>" },
      { id: 238, question: "Design Kubernetes for a global e-commerce application.", answer: "<p><strong>Architecture decisions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Multi-region active-active</strong> EKS clusters in US, EU, APAC</li><li><strong>Route53 latency routing</strong> → Global Accelerator for lowest latency</li><li><strong>DynamoDB Global Tables</strong> for session/cart data (multi-region writes)</li><li><strong>Aurora Global Database</strong> for product/order data</li><li><strong>CloudFront + S3</strong> for static assets globally</li><li><strong>ArgoCD ApplicationSets</strong> for synchronized deployments across all regions</li><li><strong>KEDA + HPA</strong> for aggressive autoscaling during flash sales</li></ul>" },
      { id: 239, question: "Design a zero-trust Kubernetes platform.", answer: "<p>Zero-trust pillars in Kubernetes:</p><ul><li><strong>Identity:</strong> IRSA/Pod Identity (no static credentials), short-lived certs via cert-manager</li><li><strong>Encryption:</strong> Istio mTLS between all services, TLS everywhere</li><li><strong>Network:</strong> Default-deny NetworkPolicies, micro-segmentation</li><li><strong>Access:</strong> RBAC with least privilege, no kubectl exec in production</li><li><strong>Supply chain:</strong> Image signing (Cosign), Kyverno enforce signed images only</li><li><strong>Runtime:</strong> Falco for anomaly detection, GuardDuty EKS monitoring</li><li><strong>Audit:</strong> All API server actions logged to CloudWatch Audit Logs</li></ul>" },
      { id: 240, question: "Design Kubernetes for PCI-DSS compliance.", answer: "<p>PCI-DSS requirements in Kubernetes:</p><ul><li><strong>Network segmentation:</strong> Separate cluster or namespace for cardholder data environment (CDE), strict NetworkPolicies</li><li><strong>Encryption:</strong> KMS encryption for Secrets at rest, mTLS for data in transit</li><li><strong>Access control:</strong> MFA for all kubectl access, RBAC with quarterly reviews</li><li><strong>Audit logging:</strong> All API server events → SIEM (Splunk)</li><li><strong>Vulnerability management:</strong> ECR scanning, Trivy in CI/CD, no critical CVEs</li><li><strong>Incident response:</strong> Falco alerts → PagerDuty for real-time threat detection</li></ul>" },
      { id: 241, question: "Design Kubernetes for HIPAA workloads.", answer: "<p>HIPAA requires protecting PHI (Protected Health Information):</p><ul><li><strong>Data encryption:</strong> EBS encrypted with KMS, Secrets Manager for credentials, TLS everywhere</li><li><strong>Access control:</strong> RBAC least privilege, AWS IAM federated access with MFA</li><li><strong>Audit trails:</strong> EKS API audit logs, CloudTrail for all AWS actions → immutable S3 storage with Object Lock</li><li><strong>Network:</strong> Private EKS cluster, VPC with no internet egress for PHI services</li><li><strong>BAA:</strong> Ensure AWS Business Associate Agreement is signed for all used AWS services</li></ul>" },
      { id: 242, question: "Design a platform supporting 50 development teams.", answer: "<p>Platform engineering for 50 teams:</p><ul><li><strong>Self-service:</strong> Internal Developer Portal (Backstage) for cluster/namespace provisioning</li><li><strong>Namespace per team</strong> with automated RBAC via Crossplane or ACK</li><li><strong>Golden paths:</strong> Curated Helm chart library + ArgoCD ApplicationSet templates</li><li><strong>Policy guardrails:</strong> Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper — enforce resource limits, image scanning, required labels</li><li><strong>Showback/chargeback:</strong> Kubecost for per-team cost visibility</li><li><strong>Observability self-service:</strong> Grafana with team-scoped dashboards, AlertManager routing per team</li></ul>" },
      { id: 243, question: "Design a highly available EKS architecture.", answer: "<p>HA EKS architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>Control plane:</strong> AWS manages HA across 3 AZs automatically</li><li><strong>Worker nodes:</strong> Managed Node Groups in 3 AZs minimum</li><li><strong>Workloads:</strong> Anti-affinity to spread replicas across AZs + TopologySpreadConstraints</li><li><strong>PodDisruptionBudgets:</strong> Ensure minimum replicas during maintenance</li><li><strong>Load balancer:</strong> ALB with health checks + Route53 failover</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> EFS for shared data (multi-AZ by default); EBS with topology-aware scheduling</li><li><strong>Target: 99.9%+ uptime</strong> for control plane and 99.95% for workloads</li></ul>" },
      { id: 244, question: "Design a multi-region Kubernetes platform.", answer: "<p>Deploy independent EKS clusters per AWS Region. Use Route 53 with latency/geolocation routing. Use DynamoDB Global Tables or Aurora Global Database for data. Synchronize deployments with ArgoCD ApplicationSets across all clusters. Use a Global Accelerator for static anycast IPs.</p><img src='assets/eks_multi_region.png' alt='Multi-Region EKS' style='width:100%;max-width:600px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;border:1px solid var(--border-color);'>" },
      { id: 245, question: "Design Kubernetes disaster recovery.", answer: "<p>DR strategy (RTO/RPO targets drive choices):</p><ul><li><strong>Backup:</strong> Velero daily snapshots to S3 (cross-region replication)</li><li><strong>GitOps:</strong> All K8s manifests in Git — restore cluster in minutes by pointing to Git repo</li><li><strong>Data:</strong> EBS snapshots, Aurora automated backups to S3, DynamoDB PITR</li><li><strong>Warm standby:</strong> Active-passive cluster in DR region with Route53 failover</li><li><strong>Active-active:</strong> Multi-region active-active for highest availability (lowest RTO)</li><li><strong>DR drills:</strong> Monthly failover tests to validate RTO/RPO</li></ul>" },
      { id: 246, question: "Design Kubernetes for AI/ML workloads.", answer: "<p>ML workloads have unique requirements:</p><ul><li><strong>GPU nodes:</strong> Karpenter NodePool with GPU instance types (p3/p4/g5); NVIDIA device plugin DaemonSet</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> FSx for Lustre (high-throughput parallel access to training data in S3)</li><li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> Gang scheduling (Volcano scheduler) for distributed training jobs</li><li><strong>Spot Instances:</strong> For training jobs with checkpointing; On-Demand for inference</li><li><strong>MLflow/KubeFlow:</strong> ML lifecycle management on Kubernetes</li><li><strong>KEDA:</strong> Scale inference deployments based on GPU utilization or request queue depth</li></ul>" },
      { id: 247, question: "Design a Kubernetes platform with GitOps.", answer: "<p>GitOps platform stack:</p><ul><li><strong>SCM:</strong> GitHub / GitLab — separate repos for app code and platform config</li><li><strong>CI:</strong> GitHub Actions — build, test, scan, sign image, push to ECR, update GitOps repo</li><li><strong>GitOps engine:</strong> ArgoCD — sync Kubernetes state from Git</li><li><strong>Secrets:</strong> External Secrets Operator → AWS Secrets Manager (never in Git)</li><li><strong>Policies:</strong> Kyverno — enforce GitOps compliance (block manual deploys)</li><li><strong>Drift alerting:</strong> ArgoCD Slack webhook on OutOfSync status</li></ul>" },
      { id: 248, question: "Design Kubernetes for financial applications.", answer: "<p>Finance applications require strict compliance and low latency:</p><ul><li><strong>Compliance:</strong> SOX/PCI-DSS controls, immutable audit logs, quarterly RBAC reviews</li><li><strong>Low latency:</strong> C5n/placement group instances for high-bandwidth trading apps; avoid noisy neighbors via dedicated node pools</li><li><strong>HA:</strong> Active-active multi-AZ, PDB ensuring quorum for all critical services</li><li><strong>Change management:</strong> GitOps with mandatory PR approvals, change freeze windows enforced via Kyverno</li><li><strong>Encryption:</strong> HSM-backed KMS keys for signing operations</li></ul>" },
      { id: 249, question: "Design Kubernetes for IoT.", answer: "<p>IoT workloads require edge and cloud coordination:</p><ul><li><strong>Edge:</strong> K3s or EKS Anywhere on edge gateways for low-latency processing</li><li><strong>Cloud EKS:</strong> Centralized data processing, ML model training, device management</li><li><strong>MQTT/Kafka:</strong> KEDA scaling consumers based on message queue depth</li><li><strong>Time-series data:</strong> Amazon Timestream or InfluxDB for device metrics</li><li><strong>AWS IoT Core → Kinesis → EKS consumers</strong> for high-volume telemetry ingestion</li><li><strong>OTA updates:</strong> GitOps-based firmware deployment to edge clusters</li></ul>" },
      { id: 250, question: "Design Kubernetes for streaming workloads.", answer: "<p>Streaming on Kubernetes (Kafka, Flink, Spark Streaming):</p><ul><li><strong>Kafka:</strong> StatefulSet with EBS gp3 volumes, 3 brokers across 3 AZs, PodDisruptionBudget minAvailable:2</li><li><strong>Flink/Spark:</strong> Kubernetes operator mode (Flink Operator, Spark Operator)</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> High-IOPS EBS (io2) for Kafka logs, EFS for checkpoints</li><li><strong>Scaling:</strong> KEDA scaling Flink taskmanagers based on Kafka consumer lag</li><li><strong>Networking:</strong> NLB for Kafka external clients, high-bandwidth EC2 instances (c5n)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 251, question: "Design Kubernetes for batch processing.", answer: "<p>Batch processing architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>Job scheduling:</strong> Kubernetes Jobs + CronJobs or Argo Workflows for DAG-based pipelines</li><li><strong>Compute:</strong> Karpenter provisioning Spot Instances for batch (with checkpointing)</li><li><strong>Queue-based scaling:</strong> KEDA scaling workers based on SQS queue depth</li><li><strong>Priority:</strong> Low PriorityClass for batch jobs so they don't compete with online services</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> S3 for input/output data, EFS for scratch space</li><li><strong>Cost:</strong> Use Savings Plans for baseline + Spot for burst</li></ul>" },
      { id: 252, question: "Design Kubernetes for event-driven architecture.", answer: "<p>Event-driven Kubernetes stack:</p><ul><li><strong>Event bus:</strong> Amazon EventBridge or Kafka (MSK) as the event backbone</li><li><strong>Consumers:</strong> EKS Pods or Lambda (for serverless consumers)</li><li><strong>KEDA:</strong> Automatically scales consumers based on EventBridge, SQS, or Kafka topic lag</li><li><strong>Dead Letter Queues:</strong> SQS DLQ for failed events with alerting</li><li><strong>Event sourcing:</strong> Store events in DynamoDB Streams or Kinesis for replay capability</li><li><strong>Saga pattern:</strong> Argo Workflows or Step Functions for distributed transaction orchestration</li></ul>" },
      { id: 253, question: "Design Kubernetes for hybrid cloud.", answer: "<p>Hybrid cloud Kubernetes:</p><ul><li><strong>EKS + EKS Anywhere:</strong> Same Kubernetes API on-prem and in cloud</li><li><strong>Connectivity:</strong> AWS Direct Connect or VPN for secure on-prem ↔ cloud communication</li><li><strong>Fleet management:</strong> ArgoCD or AWS EKS Connector for centralized management</li><li><strong>Data gravity:</strong> Process data where it resides — analytics in cloud, sensitive processing on-prem</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> AWS Storage Gateway for on-prem access to S3</li><li><strong>Service mesh:</strong> Istio cross-cluster service discovery for seamless service-to-service calls</li></ul>" },
      { id: 254, question: "Design Kubernetes for cost optimization.", answer: "<p>Cost optimization levers:</p><ul><li><strong>Right-sizing:</strong> VPA recommendations + Goldilocks to tune requests/limits</li><li><strong>Spot Instances:</strong> Karpenter with Spot for stateless workloads (60-80% savings)</li><li><strong>Autoscaling:</strong> HPA + Karpenter consolidation — never over-provision idle nodes</li><li><strong>Savings Plans:</strong> Compute Savings Plans for baseline workloads</li><li><strong>Off-hours scaling:</strong> Scale dev/staging to zero on weekends (KEDA cron trigger)</li><li><strong>Cost visibility:</strong> Kubecost or AWS Cost Explorer with K8s tags</li><li><strong>Graviton:</strong> ARM-based Graviton nodes for 20-40% better price/performance</li></ul>" },
      { id: 255, question: "Design Kubernetes with service mesh.", answer: "<p>Service mesh architecture with Istio:</p><ul><li><strong>Control plane:</strong> Istiod (Pilot + Galley + Citadel) manages configuration and certificate distribution</li><li><strong>Data plane:</strong> Envoy sidecar injected into every Pod (via MutatingWebhook)</li><li><strong>mTLS:</strong> SPIFFE/SPIRE certificates, auto-rotated every 24 hours</li><li><strong>Traffic management:</strong> VirtualServices for canary routing, DestinationRules for circuit breaking</li><li><strong>Observability:</strong> Envoy emits RED metrics per service pair → Prometheus → Grafana</li><li><strong>Gradual rollout:</strong> Start with permissive mTLS, then strict per-namespace</li></ul>" },
      { id: 256, question: "Design Kubernetes with zero downtime deployments.", answer: "<p>Zero downtime deployment checklist:</p><ul><li><strong>Readiness probes:</strong> Traffic routes to new Pods only when ready</li><li><strong>PodDisruptionBudgets:</strong> Minimum replicas maintained during updates</li><li><strong>Rolling update strategy:</strong> maxUnavailable: 0, maxSurge: 1</li><li><strong>Graceful shutdown:</strong> <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> long enough for requests to drain</li><li><strong>PreStop hook:</strong> Add sleep(5) to allow LB to deregister the Pod before SIGTERM</li><li><strong>Canary deployments:</strong> Argo Rollouts for progressive traffic shifting with auto-rollback</li></ul>" },
      { id: 257, question: "Design Kubernetes for regulatory compliance.", answer: "<p>Compliance framework on Kubernetes:</p><ul><li><strong>Policy as code:</strong> OPA Gatekeeper or Kyverno for automated compliance enforcement</li><li><strong>Audit logging:</strong> All API server actions → CloudWatch → SIEM, retained 12 months</li><li><strong>Access reviews:</strong> Quarterly RBAC reviews; automated reporting via ClusterRole/Binding exports</li><li><strong>Secrets:</strong> All secrets from Secrets Manager — no hardcoded credentials</li><li><strong>Encryption:</strong> KMS for secrets at rest, TLS/mTLS in transit</li><li><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> ECR continuous scanning, block on CRITICAL CVEs in CI/CD</li></ul>" },
      { id: 258, question: "Design Kubernetes monitoring architecture.", answer: "<p>Monitoring architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>Node metrics:</strong> Node Exporter DaemonSet → Prometheus → AMP</li><li><strong>Kubernetes metrics:</strong> kube-state-metrics + Metrics Server</li><li><strong>Application metrics:</strong> App /metrics endpoint scraped via ServiceMonitor CRD</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Amazon Managed Prometheus (serverless, auto-scaling)</li><li><strong>Visualization:</strong> Amazon Managed Grafana with pre-built K8s dashboards</li><li><strong>Alerting:</strong> Alertmanager → PagerDuty (P1/P2) + Slack (P3/P4)</li><li><strong>SLOs:</strong> Pyrra or Sloth for SLO dashboards with error budget tracking</li></ul>" },
      { id: 259, question: "Design Kubernetes logging architecture.", answer: "<p>Centralized logging architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>Collection:</strong> Fluent Bit DaemonSet on every node collecting /var/log/containers/*</li><li><strong>Enrichment:</strong> Fluent Bit adds K8s metadata (Pod name, namespace, labels)</li><li><strong>Routing:</strong> Ship to CloudWatch Logs (for AWS-native alerting) and S3 (for long-term retention + Athena queries)</li><li><strong>Search:</strong> OpenSearch (Elasticsearch) for interactive log search</li><li><strong>Retention:</strong> CloudWatch: 30 days; S3: 1 year (Glacier after 90 days)</li><li><strong>Security logs:</strong> EKS audit logs → CloudWatch → GuardDuty</li></ul>" },
      { id: 260, question: "Design Kubernetes backup architecture.", answer: "<p>Comprehensive backup strategy:</p><ul><li><strong>Configuration backup (GitOps):</strong> All K8s manifests in Git — instant restore to any cluster</li><li><strong>State backup:</strong> Velero scheduled backups (every 6 hours) → S3 with cross-region replication</li><li><strong>Volume backup:</strong> Velero CSI Volume Snapshots → EBS snapshots via AWS Backup</li><li><strong>Database backup:</strong> Aurora automated backups (35-day retention) + manual snapshots before upgrades</li><li><strong>Recovery testing:</strong> Monthly DR drills — restore to test cluster, validate data integrity</li><li><strong>RTO target:</strong> &lt;1 hour; <strong>RPO target:</strong> &lt;15 minutes</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "leadership",
    title: "12. Senior Architect & Leadership",
    questions: [
      { id: 261, question: "Why would you choose EKS over ECS?", answer: "<p>Choose EKS when:</p><ul><li>You need <strong>cloud portability</strong> (Kubernetes runs anywhere)</li><li>Access to the <strong>CNCF open-source ecosystem</strong> (Helm, Istio, Prometheus, ArgoCD)</li><li>Complex workloads requiring <strong>advanced scheduling</strong> (affinity, topology spread, custom schedulers)</li><li><strong>Multi-tenant isolation</strong> with RBAC, NetworkPolicies, namespace quotas</li><li>Your team has Kubernetes expertise</li><li>You run <strong>Hybrid or multi-cloud</strong> workloads</li></ul><p>ECS is simpler, has less overhead, and deeper AWS integration — better for smaller teams new to containers.</p>" },
      { id: 262, question: "When would you recommend ECS instead of EKS?", answer: "<p>Recommend ECS when:</p><ul><li>Small team without Kubernetes expertise — ECS has lower operational complexity</li><li>AWS-only workloads with no multi-cloud requirements</li><li>Simple container workloads that don't need advanced scheduling</li><li>AWS Fargate preference for serverless compute — ECS Fargate has better feature parity than EKS Fargate</li><li>Fast time-to-market is priority — ECS is faster to get running</li><li>Deep Service Connect, App Mesh integration needed natively</li></ul>" },
      { id: 263, question: "How do you convince leadership to migrate to Kubernetes?", answer: "<p>ROI-focused approach:</p><ul><li><strong>Cost:</strong> Show bin packing efficiency + Spot savings → typically 30-50% infra cost reduction</li><li><strong>Speed:</strong> Deployment time from weeks to minutes via GitOps</li><li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Self-healing reduces on-call burden; PDB prevents outages during deployments</li><li><strong>Developer productivity:</strong> Self-service environments, standardized toolchain</li><li><strong>Start small:</strong> Propose a pilot with 2-3 non-critical services, measure KPIs, then expand</li><li><strong>Risk mitigation:</strong> Phased migration plan with rollback capability</li></ul>" },
      { id: 264, question: "How do you estimate Kubernetes infrastructure costs?", answer: "<p>Cost estimation framework:</p><ul><li><strong>Compute:</strong> (avg CPU requests × hourly EC2 rate) + (Spot savings factor). Use Infracost or Kubecost simulator.</li><li><strong>Storage:</strong> EBS ($/GB/month × total PVC storage), EFS ($/GB transferred)</li><li><strong>Load balancing:</strong> ALB hourly rate + LCU charges</li><li><strong>Data transfer:</strong> Cross-AZ traffic at $0.01/GB</li><li><strong>EKS control plane:</strong> $0.10/hr per cluster</li><li><strong>Add buffer:</strong> 20% for overhead (system Pods, DaemonSets, reserved capacity)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 265, question: "How do you govern multiple Kubernetes clusters?", answer: "<p>Multi-cluster governance strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>Fleet management:</strong> ArgoCD ApplicationSets or Flux + GitOps for consistent config across all clusters</li><li><strong>Policy:</strong> Centralized OPA/Kyverno policies distributed via GitOps</li><li><strong>Observability:</strong> Centralized AMP + AMG for cross-cluster dashboards</li><li><strong>Access control:</strong> AWS SSO + cluster RBAC — single identity plane</li><li><strong>Upgrade management:</strong> Staggered upgrade schedule — dev first, then staging, then prod</li><li><strong>Cost governance:</strong> Kubecost multi-cluster view with per-cluster budgets</li></ul>" },
      { id: 266, question: "How do you manage 500+ microservices across EKS clusters?", answer: "<p>At 500+ microservices, automation is essential:</p><ul><li><strong>Service catalog:</strong> Backstage Internal Developer Portal for service discovery</li><li><strong>GitOps at scale:</strong> ArgoCD ApplicationSets with generators — one template deploys to all services</li><li><strong>Standardization:</strong> Company-wide Helm chart library with opinionated defaults</li><li><strong>Service mesh:</strong> Istio for automatic mTLS, distributed tracing, and circuit breaking</li><li><strong>Dependency mapping:</strong> OTel traces → service graph for understanding blast radius</li><li><strong>Ownership:</strong> Required <code>team</code> and <code>cost-center</code> labels (enforced by Kyverno)</li></ul>" },
      { id: 267, question: "How do you implement platform engineering in Kubernetes?", answer: "<p>Platform engineering builds an Internal Developer Platform (IDP):</p><ul><li><strong>Golden paths:</strong> Curated deployment templates (Helm charts, Backstage scaffolders) for common use cases</li><li><strong>Self-service:</strong> Teams provision environments without platform team involvement</li><li><strong>Guardrails:</strong> Kyverno/OPA policies enforce security and cost standards automatically</li><li><strong>Abstractions:</strong> Crossplane or ACK for self-service AWS resource provisioning from Kubernetes</li><li><strong>Developer experience:</strong> Local dev parity with Telepresence or DevSpace</li><li><strong>Feedback loops:</strong> Platform SLOs, developer satisfaction surveys</li></ul>" },
      { id: 268, question: "How do you enforce security standards across all clusters?", answer: "<p>Centralized security enforcement:</p><ul><li><strong>Policy as code:</strong> Kyverno/OPA Gatekeeper policies stored in Git, distributed via ArgoCD to all clusters</li><li><strong>Admission enforcement:</strong> Block non-compliant workloads at deploy time, not detected post-fact</li><li><strong>Image scanning:</strong> Block images with CRITICAL CVEs in admission webhook</li><li><strong>RBAC audit:</strong> Automated monthly RBAC review report via Popeye or kubectl-who-can</li><li><strong>Compliance reporting:</strong> Kyverno Policy Reports → centralized dashboard</li><li><strong>Runtime monitoring:</strong> Falco → centralized SIEM for cross-cluster threat detection</li></ul>" },
      { id: 269, question: "How do you perform Kubernetes upgrades with zero downtime?", answer: "<p>Zero-downtime upgrade process:</p><ol><li>Upgrade non-prod clusters first (dev → staging → prod)</li><li>Run <code>pluto</code> to detect deprecated APIs — fix before upgrade</li><li>Upgrade EKS control plane (AWS manages this, typically 5-10 min)</li><li>Update add-ons (VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy)</li><li>Roll out new node group version (Managed Node Groups handle graceful drain)</li><li>Validate workloads, check PDBs were respected</li><li>Monitor error rates and latency during and after upgrade</li></ol>" },
      { id: 270, question: "How do you plan a Kubernetes migration from VMs?", answer: "<p>VM to Kubernetes migration plan:</p><ol><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> Inventory all VMs, dependencies, databases, and traffic patterns</li><li><strong>Containerize:</strong> Dockerize apps — start with stateless apps (easier)</li><li><strong>Platform setup:</strong> Build EKS clusters with GitOps, observability, and security baseline</li><li><strong>Strangler Fig:</strong> Route small % of traffic to K8s while VM still runs — validate</li><li><strong>Migrate stateful apps last:</strong> Move DBs to managed services (RDS) rather than containers when possible</li><li><strong>Decommission:</strong> Remove VMs after validation period</li></ol>" },
      { id: 271, question: "How do you reduce Kubernetes operational complexity?", answer: "<p>Complexity reduction strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>EKS Auto Mode:</strong> AWS manages nodes, add-ons, and security patches</li><li><strong>Managed Add-ons:</strong> Delegate VPC CNI, CoreDNS, EBS CSI management to AWS</li><li><strong>Karpenter:</strong> Eliminate node group management</li><li><strong>Helm + ArgoCD:</strong> Standardize deployments with templated golden paths</li><li><strong>Reduce cluster count:</strong> Fewer clusters via strong multi-tenancy is simpler than managing 50 clusters</li><li><strong>Platform engineering team:</strong> Dedicated team owns the platform; product teams consume it</li></ul>" },
      { id: 272, question: "How do you standardize deployments across teams?", answer: "<p>Deployment standardization:</p><ul><li><strong>Company Helm chart:</strong> Base chart with opinionated defaults (resource limits, probes, security context) — teams only set app-specific values</li><li><strong>Backstage templates:</strong> Scaffolders create GitOps repos and initial deployment config</li><li><strong>ArgoCD ApplicationSets:</strong> Templated app deployment — consistent structure for all teams</li><li><strong>Kyverno policies:</strong> Require standard labels (team, cost-center, environment) on all Pods</li><li><strong>Preview environments:</strong> Automated per-PR environments via Argo CD + DNS automation</li></ul>" },
      { id: 273, question: "What KPIs would you define for a Kubernetes platform?", answer: "<p>Platform KPIs:</p><ul><li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Control plane availability, workload availability (P50/P95 by team)</li><li><strong>Performance:</strong> Pod scheduling latency, Karpenter provisioning time, deploy time (commit → live)</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> Mean time to patch CVEs, number of policy violations, audit compliance score</li><li><strong>Cost:</strong> Cost per team, CPU/memory utilization %, Spot usage %</li><li><strong>Developer experience:</strong> Time to first deploy for new service, platform NPS score</li><li><strong>Operational:</strong> MTTD, MTTR for platform incidents</li></ul>" },
      { id: 274, question: "How do you handle production incidents?", answer: "<p>Incident response process:</p><ol><li><strong>Detect:</strong> Alert fires (PagerDuty), on-call engineer acknowledges within 5 min</li><li><strong>Triage:</strong> Assess severity (P1-P4), page additional responders for P1</li><li><strong>Mitigate first:</strong> Rollback deployment, scale up, re-route traffic — restore service before root cause analysis</li><li><strong>Communicate:</strong> Update status page, stakeholder communication every 30 min</li><li><strong>Resolve:</strong> Fix underlying issue, validate resolution</li><li><strong>Post-mortem:</strong> Blameless RCA within 48 hours, document action items</li></ol>" },
      { id: 275, question: "How do you perform root cause analysis after an outage?", answer: "<p>Blameless post-mortem process:</p><ul><li><strong>Timeline reconstruction:</strong> Collect all signals (metrics, logs, traces, alerts) into a chronological timeline</li><li><strong>Five Whys:</strong> Drill down to the root cause systematically</li><li><strong>Contributing factors:</strong> Identify all factors that contributed (not just the trigger)</li><li><strong>Action items:</strong> Each finding → specific, time-bound improvement action with an owner</li><li><strong>Detection gap:</strong> Why wasn't this caught earlier? Improve monitoring/testing</li><li><strong>Prevention:</strong> What process/technology change prevents recurrence?</li></ul>" },
      { id: 276, question: "What are the biggest Kubernetes anti-patterns you've seen?", answer: "<ul><li><strong>No resource requests/limits:</strong> Causes noisy neighbor problems and random OOMKills</li><li><strong>Running as root:</strong> Security risk — always use <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code></li><li><strong>Single replica for critical services:</strong> No HA; any restart causes downtime</li><li><strong>Latest image tag:</strong> Not reproducible; breaks GitOps — use immutable SHA or version tags</li><li><strong>No liveness/readiness probes:</strong> Traffic goes to unhealthy Pods</li><li><strong>Secrets in environment variables:</strong> Visible in container inspect and logs</li><li><strong>Manual kubectl in production:</strong> Bypasses GitOps, creates drift</li></ul>" },
      { id: 277, question: "What trade-offs have you made in Kubernetes architecture?", answer: "<p>Common trade-offs:</p><ul><li><strong>Multi-tenancy vs isolation:</strong> Shared clusters reduce cost but require more security controls vs separate clusters being simpler but expensive</li><li><strong>Service mesh vs simplicity:</strong> Istio gives mTLS and observability but adds latency and complexity</li><li><strong>Spot vs reliability:</strong> Spot saves 70% cost but requires stateless, interruptible workloads</li><li><strong>GitOps strictness vs velocity:</strong> Strict pull-request workflows improve safety but slow down emergency hotfixes</li><li><strong>DRY helm charts vs autonomy:</strong> Central chart standardizes but limits team flexibility</li></ul>" },
      { id: 278, question: "How do you evaluate new Kubernetes technologies?", answer: "<p>Technology evaluation framework:</p><ol><li><strong>Problem fit:</strong> Does it solve a real problem we have? Avoid shiny object syndrome.</li><li><strong>Maturity:</strong> CNCF graduation level, GitHub stars, production adoption at similar scale</li><li><strong>Operational cost:</strong> Who maintains this? What's the upgrade path?</li><li><strong>Proof of concept:</strong> Small-scale test in dev cluster with realistic load</li><li><strong>Team capability:</strong> Do we have skills to operate this? What's the learning curve?</li><li><strong>Exit strategy:</strong> Can we remove it if it doesn't work out?</li></ol>" },
      { id: 279, question: "What does a production-ready Kubernetes platform look like?", answer: "<p>Production readiness checklist:</p><ul><li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Multi-AZ, Karpenter autoscaling, Spot + On-Demand mix</li><li><strong>Delivery:</strong> GitOps (ArgoCD), image signing, automated scanning in CI/CD</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> RBAC + IRSA, NetworkPolicies, Pod Security Standards Restricted, runtime monitoring (Falco)</li><li><strong>Observability:</strong> Metrics (AMP/Grafana) + Logs (Fluent Bit/CloudWatch) + Traces (X-Ray/Jaeger)</li><li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Probes, PDBs, graceful termination, tested DR runbooks</li><li><strong>Cost:</strong> Kubecost dashboards, Savings Plans, idle resource cleanup</li></ul>" },
      { id: 280, question: "What would your Kubernetes roadmap look like for the next three years?", answer: "<p><strong>Year 1: Stabilize</strong></p><ul><li>Migrate all workloads to EKS, implement GitOps + ArgoCD</li><li>Establish observability stack (AMP, AMG, X-Ray)</li><li>Baseline security posture (RBAC, NetworkPolicies, image scanning)</li></ul><p><strong>Year 2: Optimize</strong></p><ul><li>Karpenter + Spot — 40% cost reduction</li><li>Service mesh (Istio) for mTLS and traffic management</li><li>Internal Developer Platform (Backstage) for self-service</li></ul><p><strong>Year 3: Scale</strong></p><ul><li>Multi-region active-active, global load balancing</li><li>AI/ML platform on GPU nodes</li><li>Platform as a product with SLOs and developer NPS</li></ul>" }
    ]
  }
];
